## 10. Hard rules summary
551

552
1. Main thread orchestrates. **No NESTED orchestrator** — an
553
   orchestrator or an implementer never spawns another orchestrator.
554
   Scope: this rule and the § 1 anti-pattern date to `72af7045`
555
   (2026-05-17), **46 days** before the `orchestrator` / `steward` agent
556
   types existed (`31a73401`, 2026-07-02); both are **silent on a
557
   Steward-commissioned orchestrator, not prohibitive** (§ 1.1).
558
2. Main thread spawns implementers via the Agent tool,
559
   `subagent_type: implementer`. Worktree is main-thread-owned
560
   (`git worktree add`); never use Agent isolation. (The `claude -p`
561
   subprocess pattern is retired — § 2.)
562
3. Per-spawn facts (worktree path, branch, baseline SHA, output path)
563
   passed in the Agent prompt. Always. Durable role lives in the agent
564
   definition.
565
4. The `implementer` agent type omits Agent/Task — the physical
566
   anti-chain guard. Never add them to its tool list.
567
5. Codex reviewer is read-only. Always `--sandbox read-only`.
568
6. Verdict promoted by main thread (codex can't write).
569
7. Cherry-pick to mainline, never merge from WT branch.
570
8. Never override BLOCK. Override CONCERN only when structural or
571
   prompt-induced.
572
9. Worktree cleanup after phase family closes (§ 11).
573
10. **One writer per checkout.** Never run more than one file-mutating
574
    session or subagent in the same working tree at once — each concurrent
575
    writer gets its own worktree (cut off `origin/main`), or they are
576
    serialized. Read-only subagents may share a checkout. Push explicit
577
    branch refs (HEAD-independent); verify-from-git after concurrent work
578
    (§ 13).
579

