{"ts": "2026-07-03T20:52:05.224Z", "seq": 1, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "EXP-S (#2, 0.8.14) coexisting-index / multi-index-write substrate must not foreclose the OPP-12 projection registry that will later sit on it — design the multi-index write so a declarative filterable/rankable/searchable registry can wrap it without a substrate reshape", "area": "engine/substrate", "epistemic": "verified", "id": "TC-1", "owner": "orchestrator", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["opp:OPP-12", "plan:dev/plans/plan-0.8.14.md", "file:dev/design/record-lifecycle-protocol/projection-registry-and-async-embed.md", "git:83ef13fc"], "body": "Grounded caveat for the 0.8.14 EXP-S build (Slice-0 ADR + Slice-5 keystone). OPP-12's engine-owned projection registry (filterable/rankable/searchable declarations; same-txn FTS/filter, async vector via the existing worker + the atomic dense_readiness flip) is architecturally a DECLARATIVE FRONT-END over exactly the coexisting-index / incremental multi-index write that EXP-S builds -- EXP-S is the substrate the projection registry will sit on (OPP-12 lands >=0.9.x). No dependency edge forces ordering, and EXP-S touches NONE of OPP-12's surface (verified: plan-0.8.14 has no SearchHit / lifecycle / projection scope). But the Slice-0 substrate ADR should design the multi-index write so a later projection registry can WRAP it without a substrate reshape (don't foreclose per-kind declarative projection specs or the async-vector dense_readiness flip). Re-check at OPP-12 scheduling. See [[opp12-record-lifecycle-protocol]]."}
