{"ts": "2026-07-09T23:19:10.270Z", "seq": 21, "kind": "todo", "summary": "TC-11 (NEW, HITL 2026-07-09; master F-23 GUARDRAIL): 0.8.20 (OPP-12 Phase-2) MUST pin the doc-seeded 'h:' end-state BEFORE Phase-2 proceeds -- is doc-seeded/anonymous SearchHit.id 'h:<content-hash>' TERMINAL-FOREVER, FORWARD-MINT-ONLY (new anonymous nodes get l: surrogates, old stay h:), or BACKFILLED (h:->l: remap of the existing ~18k doc-seeded rows + real-gold remap)? The genuine long-run risk (surfaced by the 0.8.19 Slice-0 adversarial audit) is a PERMANENT SPLIT: pre-0.8.20 doc-seeded h: vs post-0.8.20 anonymous l: for identical re-ingested content. This exists INDEPENDENT of 1a's timing (1a correctly deferred the decision to where the natural-key/projection registry lives). LEADING HYPOTHESIS (audit inference, confirm at Phase-2 design): 'h:' is TERMINAL -- doc-chunks are NOT EntityTypeSpec entities, so the C-1 anonymous-entity surrogate path may not apply to them -> h: terminal -> 1a fully safe, no backfill ever. ACTION: de-stale plan-0.8.20.md (currently holds pre-F-19 dep-migrations content) + author OPP-12 Phase-2 ladder including this end-state decision record. Owner: 0.8.20 Slice-0.", "decider": "hitl", "kind_detail": "phase2-docseeded-endstate", "release": "0.8.20", "refs": ["plan:dev/plans/0.8.6-0.8.16-PROGRAM-SEQUENCING.md"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-10T21:02:42.994Z", "seq": 22, "kind": "reconcile", "summary": "TC-8 (OPP-12 C-2 SearchHit.id swap: write_cursor+stable_id -> typed IdSpace) RESOLVED — shipped in 0.8.19 Phase-1 (label-only, origin/main 578fe20b; master F-24; OPP-12 sub-ledger seq 9). u64->IdSpace {space,value}; to_prefixed() == prior stable_id byte-for-byte => real-gold keying a true no-op (no gold-remap); breaking surface built label-only, PUBLISHES at 0.8.20. TC-11 (0.8.20 doc-seeded h: end-state pin + de-stale plan-0.8.20) REMAINS OPEN and is the FIRST Phase-2 step. NEW carries for the 0.8.20 commission, from the rubric-eval v3 pilot findings on 0.8.19 (HARD GATE PASS 12/12, 84.4%): (a) G6 — run Py/TS parity BEFORE land as one unit on any surface-touching slice (0.8.19 ran X1 green AFTER land due to a native-import env trap; treat an env trap as a landing blocker, not a follow-up); (b) TC-RUBRIC-7 — persist every codex §9 review transcript to a durable release-namespaced path (e.g. scratchpad/codex/0.8.20/slice-N-review.txt) so B1/B2 independent re-verification is possible after the fact; (c) TC-RUBRIC-5 / A6 — dedicated checkout/worktree per orchestration (the shared-checkout guard; see the steward guardrail-fix proposal). Fold (a)+(b) into the 0.8.20 X0 process gate.", "decider": "steward", "release": "0.8.20", "refs": ["git:578fe20b", "seq:21", "file:dev/design/record-lifecycle-protocol/OPP-12-sub-ledger.jsonl"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-11T15:40:59.056Z", "seq": 23, "kind": "consideration", "summary": "0.8.20 Slice-0 ERASURE PRIMITIVE inputs (HITL steer 2026-07-11, reframes the GDPR/purge question). KEY REFRAME: the erasure axis is PROVENANCE (source_id / excise_source), NOT the l:/h: id-space -> pin A is ORTHOGONAL to erasure and survives regardless. Slice-0 must ask 'is excise_source a COMPLETE erasure primitive, and is provenance MANDATORY?' with THREE concrete work-items (defects, not design debates): (1) ONE shared row-owned delete-set consumed by BOTH purge_inner and excise_source_inner -- the bug is two erasure verbs maintaining the target-table list INDEPENDENTLY, so the next projection table reopens the hole (per memory: excise_source misses the content-storing search_index_v2 => body survives erasure); the RED test asserts on RAW TABLE CONTENTS, not search results (leak is data-at-rest, never surfaces in results). (2) Fix ingest provenance -- use the caller's d.source_doc_id, NOT the model's echo (straight bug fix). (3) source_id MANDATORY (String, not Option) on PreparedWrite::Node/Edge -- this is where the breaking-changes lever pays, converting erase-by-provenance from best-effort to TOTAL; PLUS expose excise_source in the napi/py SDKs (else Memex has NO erasure capability at all). THE ONE HITL DECISION (not a fix): pre-change NULL-provenance rows get a BACKFILL or a documented DATED BOUNDARY. See memory [[erasure-axis-is-provenance-excise-source-gap]].", "decider": "hitl", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "0", "refs": ["seq:22"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-12T00:00:00.000Z", "seq": 24, "kind": "decision", "voice": "FATHOM", "status": "RATIFIED", "summary": "TC-11 CLOSED + seq-23 CORRECTION (HITL 2026-07-12; master F-25). (1) TC-11 RATIFIED = pin A, TERMINAL-FOREVER: anonymous/doc-seeded nodes stay h:<content-hash> PERMANENTLY; structural-lifecycle-contract.md 2(ii) is OVERRULED; the anonymous-surrogate leg is CANCELLED, not deferred (a surrogate serves ONLY registry-admitted governed entities, decided at write time; a stored row's id-space is never re-derived). Accepted corollary: an anonymous row + a later governed row for the same thing BOTH stay active and BOTH surface in search (NULL never collides with the partial-unique index) -- the engine does not dedupe them. (2) CORRECTION TO seq 23 -- its work-item (3) said 'source_id mandatory + excise_source IN THE SDKs'. The excise_source-in-SDK half is WRONG and is RETRACTED: excise_source is the RECOVERY seam (REQ-026, built to excise a corrupt ingest) and is named in REQ-037 as SDK-forbidden. But 'CLI-only' is ALSO wrong as a resolution: NO CLI SHIPS to SDK consumers (the wheel declares no [project.scripts]; npm no 'bin'), so erasure is UNREACHABLE. Real policy = 'RECOVERY-NAMED verbs are CLI-only', NOT 'destructive => CLI-only' -- proof: purge(logical_id) is ALREADY an SDK verb (py:1297, napi:1102) despite being named in REQ-037, because AC-041 tests only the REQ-054 five-name denylist. RULING: excise_source stays CLI-only; ADD erase_source(source_id) as a first-class SDK LIFECYCLE verb (not a recovery name => AC-041 stays GREEN, denylist stays five), one shared engine path. REQ-037 amended with a lawful-erasure carve-out; purge_logical_id struck from its forbidden list (shipped code already contradicted it). (3) ERASURE DEFECT CLASS WIDENED: the row-owned projection table list is implicit and duplicated FIVE times, not two. search_index_v2 (content-storing FTS5, holds the body verbatim) is written by project_canonical_node_row:11806 and deleted ONLY by purge:6229 -- excise_source_inner:6398, rebuild_shadow_state:6515, and reproject_search_index_after_tokenizer_upgrade:9515 NEVER touch it => erased bodies survive on disk; the table accumulates every body ever written; and v1/v2 diverge after a tokenizer upgrade. Also: NO edge projector exists (edge projection is inlined in commit_batch) so projector-replay rebuild must cover edges; truncate_wal() EXISTS (:6379, typed Busy) but purge/excise never call it; telemetry retains l: ids post-erasure (:4462); op-store has no record-level delete (:10083 is a cap sweep). RED tests MUST assert on RAW TABLE CONTENTS -- both FTS read paths gate on canonical_nodes, so a test that SEARCHES for the excised text PASSES on the broken code. (4) plan-0.8.20.md DE-STALED + AUTHORED (dep-migration runbook removed -> owed to plan-0.8.22.md). Design of record: dev/design/0.8.20-erasure-and-h-end-state-v4.md (codex 9 rounds 1-3).", "refs": ["file:dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md", "file:dev/design/0.8.20-erasure-and-h-end-state-v4.md", "file:dev/plans/0.8.6-0.8.16-PROGRAM-SEQUENCING.md#F-25", "seq:23"], "opp": "OPP-12", "cx": "TC-11"}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T03:35:38.066Z", "seq": 25, "kind": "observation", "summary": "eu7 is a CPU SAME-BACKEND gate: a GPU-run eu7 number is NOT comparable to the 0.90 floor and manufactures a FALSE REGRESSION. Verified at 0.8.20 Slice-0 from run artifacts: CPU (GA-signoff-eu7-remeasure-20260608T172804Z.json) n=7667 vector-stage recall@10 = 0.8960 CI [0.8640, 0.9250] => PASS on the one-sided gate (ci_hi >= 0.90). GPU (dev/plans/runs/0.8.14-slice-20-eu7-gpu-run-20260705T205222Z.log:73) n=7667 = 0.8330 CI [0.8010, 0.8640] => FAIL. The delta is a cross-backend quantization artifact, NOT a code regression (it is the TC-5 floor re-baseline driver, scheduled 0.8.23). CONSEQUENCE: the standing HITL GPU-eval mandate (repo-must-use-gpu-for-eval-when-3090-room) DOES NOT APPLY to the eu7 recall gate -- by that mandate's own 'fidelity-safe' caveat. Any release taking an eu7 basis decision (0.8.20 R-20-EU7 at Slice 40; TC-5 at 0.8.23) MUST capture and compare on CPU only, and must compare against a CPU reference. Running eu7 on the 3090s to 'honor the mandate' would wrongly force a re-baseline.", "cx": "TC-12", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "0", "status": "OPEN", "refs": ["file:dev/design/0.8.20-slice0-erasure-design.md#8", "file:dev/plans/runs/STATUS-0.8.20.md#6", "seq:24"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T03:35:38.096Z", "seq": 26, "kind": "observation", "summary": "eu7 harness has TWO vacuous-green hazards; any release running eu7 must guard both. (1) THE DOCUMENTED RUN COMMAND DOES NOT WORK. src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/tests/eu7_real_corpus_ac.rs:85-86 documents 'AGENT_LONG=1 cargo test -p fathomdb-engine --features default-embedder --test eu7_real_corpus_ac'. That fails with EXIT 101: 'target eu7_real_corpus_ac in package fathomdb-engine requires the features: operator'. Working invocation: --features default-embedder,operator. Fix the docstring (0.8.20 Slice 5 owns it). (2) THE CORPUS IS UNREACHABLE FROM A LINKED WORKTREE. data/corpus-data/ is gitignored (.gitignore:9) and exists ONLY in the primary checkout (2.1 GB). From a worktree, load_subset_or_skip finds nothing, the test prints [skip] and returns EXIT 0 -- a full vacuous green having measured nothing. Workaround used at 0.8.20 Slice-0: symlink data/corpus-data from the primary checkout into the worktree and exclude it via .git/info/exclude (no tracked file changes). GUARD REQUIRED: assert a NON-SKIP WITNESS (e.g. the EU7_SETUP real_docs=N line) rather than trusting exit 0. Compounded by the known trap background-exit-masks-real-exit: a trailing echo around the cargo invocation reported exit 0 while the real exit was 101 -- this recurred live during Slice-0.", "cx": "TC-13", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "0", "status": "OPEN", "refs": ["file:src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/tests/eu7_real_corpus_ac.rs", "file:dev/plans/runs/STATUS-0.8.20.md#6.2"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T03:36:01.033Z", "seq": 27, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "AC ID-SPACE ABOVE AC-076 IS RESERVED -- do not mint new ACs by taking 'max AC id + 1'. Verified at 0.8.20 Slice-0: the highest DEFINED, NON-RESERVED acceptance criterion is AC-076 (dev/acceptance.md:1147). AC-077 (:1286) is a RESERVED PLACEHOLDER, explicitly 'not yet a gate; no fabricated numbers', held for the IR-eval IR-1/IR-2 initiative (agentic IR / evidence-recall, maps to REQ-067, all thresholds TBD). AC-078 is CONDITIONALLY reserved to the SAME initiative -- :1297 reads '+ AC-078... only if the consensus splits the measure'. TRAP: a naive AC-036\nAC-037\nAC-019\nAC-077\nAC-077\nAC-075\nAC-076\nAC-075\nAC-077\nAC-078 returns AC-078 from that PROSE and will mislead an agent into minting a colliding id. plan-0.8.20.md section 3 states 'AC ceiling today = AC-077' and that new ACs 'continue from' it -- following that literally collides with a live reservation. 0.8.20 Slice-0 RECOMMENDS minting from AC-079 (HITL decision owed). Any future release minting ACs must read the reservation note at dev/acceptance.md:1286-1300 first, and per the locked-acceptance.md policy should prefer tracking by requirement id + TDD test name over minting per-feature ACs at all.", "cx": "TC-14", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "0", "status": "OPEN", "refs": ["file:dev/acceptance.md", "file:dev/design/0.8.20-slice0-erasure-design.md#5"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T03:36:01.065Z", "seq": 28, "kind": "consideration", "summary": "THE ERASURE AUDIT TRAIL IS DESTRUCTIBLE -- refutes the v4 design of record. dev/design/0.8.20-erasure-and-h-end-state-v4.md section 3.6 asserts excise_source_audit 'retains source_id PERMANENTLY (:6479) -- by design; that is the audit trail', and plan-0.8.20.md section 8 repeats it as the justification for the non-PII source_id rule. VERIFIED FALSE at 4ca70ba6: the audit row is written into operational_mutations (engine lib.rs:6479), and enforce_provenance_retention (:10070) sweeps THAT SAME TABLE cap-first, oldest-id-first, with NO COLLECTION FILTER (:10083-10089: DELETE FROM operational_mutations WHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM operational_mutations ORDER BY id LIMIT ?1)). Erasure audit rows are swept like any other op-store row. TWO CONSEQUENCES: (1) ACCOUNTABILITY GAP -- demonstrating THAT an erasure occurred is a distinct obligation from performing it; a retention sweep can silently destroy the proof. (2) COLLISION WITH WORK ITEM 9 -- excise_collection_record operates on the op-store, the SAME table the audit rows live in, so the erasure record and the payloads being erased share one retention pool. The non-PII source_id rule STILL STANDS (an unswept audit row may still hold it) but its stated rationale is argued from a false premise and must be corrected. RECOMMENDED (HITL, decision owed): exempt the erasure-audit collection from enforce_provenance_retention by filtering the sweep by collection, and state the audit-durability guarantee explicitly. This is a RETENTION-POLICY change, not a pure defect repair, hence HITL-gated.", "cx": "TC-15", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "OPEN", "refs": ["file:dev/design/0.8.20-slice0-erasure-design.md#2", "seq:24"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T03:44:59.060Z", "seq": 29, "kind": "todo", "summary": "THE PUBLISH DRY-RUN GUARD IS DEAD AND HAS BEEN RED SINCE 0.8.14 -- fix before the first real publish. scripts/tests/test_actionlint_fixture.sh:53-56 asserts that every publish-rust-<tier> job block in .github/workflows/release.yml contains the literal 'cargo publish --dry-run -p '. It FAILS on the FIRST tier (t1-embedder-api), which makes ./scripts/agent-test.sh exit 1 wholesale. dev/plans/runs/0.8.14-slice-40-output.json:23 already recorded this as 'the only remaining agent-verify red' -- it has now been carried unfixed across 0.8.14/0.8.16/0.8.18/0.8.19 into 0.8.20. DIAGNOSIS (verified at 4ca70ba6): the WORKFLOW IS FINE, THE TEST IS STALE. release.yml:248 no longer inlines the cargo command; it delegates to 'bash scripts/release/cargo-publish-if-new.sh --dry-run fathomdb-embedder-api', and that helper does forward to 'cargo publish --dry-run -p <crate>' (cargo-publish-if-new.sh:14). So publish BEHAVIOR is intact and this is not a publish defect. THE REAL HARM IS THE MASKED GATE: a permanently-red assertion is a gate nobody reads, so any GENUINE publish-wiring regression would now be invisible -- and 0.8.20 is the FIRST REAL PUBLISH (manifests 0.8.9 -> 0.8.20, cargo order embedder -> engine, and a pushed v* tag auto-fires REAL crates/PyPI/npm). Same class as perf-recall-gates-masked. FIX (Slice 40, R-20-PUB): re-point the assertion at the delegation -- assert the job invokes cargo-publish-if-new.sh with --dry-run AND that the helper forwards to cargo publish --dry-run -p -- so the guard is live again, then confirm ./scripts/agent-test.sh is GREEN before the publish gate.", "cx": "TC-16", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "40", "status": "OPEN", "refs": ["file:scripts/tests/test_actionlint_fixture.sh", "file:.github/workflows/release.yml", "file:dev/plans/runs/0.8.14-slice-40-output.json"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T03:45:24.246Z", "seq": 30, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "SLICE 5's SourceId NEWTYPE WILL BREAK THE eu7 HARNESS (and every other in-tree PreparedWrite construction) -- budget for it. R-20-E3 / v4 section 3.2 replaces 'source_id: Option<String>' with a mandatory SourceId newtype on the public PreparedWrite, making 'no provenance' INEXPRESSIBLE. That is the point of the change -- but it means EVERY existing in-tree construction of PreparedWrite with 'source_id: None' stops compiling. CONFIRMED INSTANCE: src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/tests/eu7_real_corpus_ac.rs:405 seeds the real-corpus haystack with 'source_id: None, logical_id: None'. eu7 is the ANN-fidelity gate feeding R-20-EU7 at Slice 40, so if Slice 5 lands the newtype without updating this harness, the Slice-40 eu7 basis decision cannot even be measured -- the test will not build. v4 section 3.2 only anticipated 'two internal callers (:5186, :5209, the doc(hidden) poison fixture)'; the TEST-side callers were not enumerated. ACTION for Slice 5: sweep ALL constructions of PreparedWrite across src/ AND tests/ (not just the two v4 names), give each a real SourceId, and re-verify the eu7 harness still BUILDS before landing. Cheap to do with the change, expensive to discover at Slice 40.", "cx": "TC-17", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "OPEN", "refs": ["file:src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/tests/eu7_real_corpus_ac.rs", "file:dev/design/0.8.20-erasure-and-h-end-state-v4.md#3.2"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T03:45:24.280Z", "seq": 31, "kind": "observation", "summary": "PROCESS: committing a codex transcript into the reviewed range pollutes the NEXT review's diff. TC-RUBRIC-7 requires persisting every codex section-9 transcript to a durable release-namespaced path. At 0.8.20 Slice-0 the transcript was committed to dev/plans/runs/codex/0.8.20/ and the fix-1 RE-review was then run with the same '--base 4ca70ba6'. Because the base was unchanged, the re-review's diff INCLUDED the previously-committed transcript file, so codex re-read its own prior findings as though they were part of the change under review -- the old [P2] finding text reappeared verbatim in the new transcript, which is easy to misread as 'the finding was not fixed'. It was fixed (verified independently: pre-fix exit 128, post-fix exit 0 under codex's own reproduction), and codex's actual terminal verdict was PASS. MITIGATIONS (pick one, for every later slice): (a) re-review against a base that already contains the prior transcript, (b) persist transcripts on a path excluded from the review scope, or (c) commit transcripts only AFTER the slice's final review round. Recommend (c) as the simplest: it keeps the reviewed diff to real code+docs and still satisfies TC-RUBRIC-7 at slice close. Also note the transcript path was pinned as .log (not .md) to match the existing 0.8.16-slice-*-codex-review-*.log convention and stay out of markdownlint scope.", "cx": "TC-18", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "0", "status": "OPEN", "refs": ["file:dev/plans/runs/STATUS-0.8.20.md#5.1"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T04:09:21.079Z", "seq": 32, "kind": "observation", "summary": "ROOT CAUSE: the eu7 harness CANNOT COMPLETE ON CPU on this box -- BATCH=256 exceeds its own hardcoded 600s drain timeout at the measured embed rate. Measured at 4ca70ba6 by bisecting N (three runs): n=7667 FAILED, n=200 FAILED, n=20 PASSED. All three failures are identical: panic at eu7_real_corpus_ac.rs:414 'seed drain (batch): Scheduler' == engine.drain(600_000) -> wait_for_idle timed out -> EngineError::Scheduler. THE WORKER IS NOT WEDGED: n=20 passed cleanly (exit 0, vector_stage recall@10 = 1.0000, 258.54s) so the embed/projection path is FUNCTIONALLY CORRECT. It is THROUGHPUT. The n=20 run reports seed_ms=111670 for 20 docs = 0.179 docs/sec, which is ~7.3x SLOWER than the 1.3 docs/sec the harness docstring assumes (:97-99). At 0.179 docs/s a BATCH of 256 needs ~1430s, so it can NEVER drain inside the hardcoded 600_000 ms -- the harness is STRUCTURALLY unable to run here regardless of N, because failure happens on the FIRST batch. Full 7667-doc seed would need ~11.9 HOURS even if the timeout were raised. NOT a weights problem (cache complete: config.json + tokenizer.json + model.safetensors present) and not CPU contention (load 4.5 of 24 cores). OPTIONS for Slice 40: (a) reduce BATCH 256 -> 64 (64/0.179 = 358s, fits inside 600s) or make BATCH/timeout env-tunable -- minimal, surgical, does NOT change measurement semantics; (b) raise the drain timeout and accept a ~12h CPU run; (c) investigate the 7x throughput shortfall vs the documented rate, which may itself be a real CPU-embed regression worth catching. RECOMMEND (a)+(c). NOTE THE TENSION: TC-12 forbids running eu7 on GPU for comparability, and CPU cannot finish -- so R-20-EU7 has NO runnable path until this is fixed. Slice 40 must not treat this as optional. SIDE-EFFECT HAZARD: the harness WRITES dev/plans/runs/eu7-latest-measurements.json into the repo on every run, so a reduced-N scouting run silently produces a file that LOOKS authoritative (the n=20 run wrote recall=1.0000 there). Do not commit it from a scouting run.", "cx": "TC-19", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "40", "status": "OPEN", "refs": ["file:src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/tests/eu7_real_corpus_ac.rs", "seq:26"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T23:49:04.707Z", "seq": 33, "kind": "observation", "summary": "R-20-EU7 is CLOSED BY HITL (zero runs authorized) but the prohibition is UNENFORCEABLE: eu7_real_corpus_ac has no #[ignore] and no env gate, so a bare 'cargo test --workspace' or 'cargo test -p fathomdb-engine --features operator' WILL execute it. Slice 5 complied only by running targets individually. Per fix-the-tooling-not-the-actor this wants a mechanical guard -- but a naive #[ignore] creates the OPPOSITE hazard at Slice 40, where eu7 IS wanted and would then silently never run (TC-13 vacuous-green class). Needs a Steward/HITL decision on the guard's shape, not a unilateral fix.", "id": "TC-20", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:00b46b84"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T23:49:04.735Z", "seq": 34, "kind": "observation", "summary": "PRE-EXISTING (not 0.8.20): pr_g10_reranker_ce has NOT COMPILED under --features default-reranker since 0.8.19's SearchHit.id u64->IdSpace swap; file verified byte-identical to baseline, so this is not a Slice-5 regression. Root cause of its survival: the release DoD full-workspace gate (cargo clippy/check --workspace --all-targets) does NOT cover feature-gated targets, so default-reranker targets are invisible to it. Two gaps: fix the compile break, and decide whether the DoD gate must fan out over feature combinations.", "id": "TC-21", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:00b46b84"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T23:49:04.758Z", "seq": 35, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "STRUCTURAL: a BREAKING change to the write path cannot be X1-verified from an orchestration worktree. maturin develop / pip install -e are prohibited there (they rebind the shared .venv), so the Python native extension cannot be built or executed -- Slice 5c's SourceId newtype broke ~50 Python fixtures that could only be statically checked (py_compile + ruff + AST audit), never run. TypeScript is NOT subject to this: 'npm install' in src/ts is directory-local, creates no shared state, and gave a real tsc + node --test green (170 passing). Rule of thumb for future breaking slices: TS is worktree-verifiable, Python is NOT, and a main-tree pytest run is owed before land. 0.8.19 already taught that an env trap is a landing blocker, not a follow-up.", "id": "TC-22", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:875017a2"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T23:49:04.781Z", "seq": 36, "kind": "observation", "summary": "TOOLING: implementer closure output.json artifacts left UNTRACKED (orchestration.md section 8 permits this, orchestrator promotes later) are destroyed by routine git hygiene. Happened TWICE in Slice 5: one implementer ran 'git checkout --' on uncommitted lib.rs and lost a whole implementation; another ran 'git stash push -u' + 'git stash drop' and swept away three prior sub-slices' untracked output.json (recovered byte-identical from the dropped stash's third parent, verified via git hash-object). Fix the tooling: have implementers COMMIT output.json as their final commit rather than leaving it untracked, so the closure witness is not destructible by ordinary git commands.", "id": "TC-23", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open"}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T23:49:04.804Z", "seq": 37, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "BEHAVIOR CHANGE to a shipped knob: enforce_provenance_retention's 'cap' now bounds SWEEPABLE rows, not physical rows in operational_mutations. Slice 5 exempts two collections from the sweep -- the erasure-audit collection (per HITL ruling 'there must be an auditable record of deletion event', defect D-A) and the new pending-redaction intent queue (sweeping it would re-create the P2 false-success defect via cap pressure). Consequence: an operator who sized 'cap' against physical row count will see the table grow beyond it by the exempt collections. Deliberate, but it needs a changelog line and any operator-facing retention docs updated.", "id": "TC-24", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:18197495"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T23:49:04.827Z", "seq": 38, "kind": "observation", "summary": "VACUOUS-GREEN HAZARD: src/rust/crates/fathomdb/tests/sdk_only_erasure.rs is '#![cfg(not(feature = \"operator\"))]' by design -- it proves an SDK-ONLY consumer with no CLI/operator surface can erase (R-20-E4). Consequence: any feature-unified run (cargo test --workspace, or anything enabling 'operator') compiles it to ZERO tests and reports success having asserted nothing. It requires the explicit invocation 'cargo test -p fathomdb --test sdk_only_erasure'. CI must carry that invocation explicitly or the R-20-E4 guarantee is untested.", "id": "TC-25", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:4b78658d"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-19T23:49:04.851Z", "seq": 39, "kind": "todo", "summary": "DESIGN TEXT CORRECTION OWED: plan-0.8.20 R-20-E8 and the v4/v5 designs state the legacy provenance backfill is gated on 'logical_id IS NULL ONLY', rationale being that governed rows stay purge-addressable by logical_id. codex section 9 proved that rationale is TRUE FOR NODES and FALSE FOR EDGES -- purge_inner resolves targets exclusively via 'SELECT state FROM canonical_nodes WHERE logical_id = ?1', so an edge's logical_id is only a supersession identity and confers NO purge-addressability. A legacy edge with source_id NULL and logical_id NOT NULL was therefore erasable by no verb at all. Shipped fix (step 21): nodes keep the logical_id IS NULL gate; edges backfill on source_id IS NULL alone. TC-11 pin NOT affected (only source_id is written, never logical_id). The design/plan prose still states the unqualified rule and must be corrected.", "id": "TC-26", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:989fd7ef"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T00:51:27.551Z", "seq": 40, "kind": "observation", "summary": "HARD-RULE TRAP: 'maturin develop' can fire AUTONOMOUSLY from a worktree without any agent issuing it. src/python/tests/conftest.py::_ensure_test_hooks_binding (line ~118) shells out to 'maturin develop' to build the test-hooks extension. So merely running the Python suite from an orchestration worktree attempts the one thing the build-isolation rule forbids (it rebinds the SHARED .venv). Observed live in Slice 5 fix-3. It failed harmlessly ONLY because the interpreter was not an activated virtualenv (conftest guards on that); with an activated venv it would have rebound the shared .venv to the worktree. Orchestrator verified no damage: /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb/.venv/.../fathomdb.pth mtime still 2026-07-09, still pointing at the MAIN repo. Per fix-the-tooling-not-the-actor, conftest needs an explicit guard that refuses to run maturin when CWD is a linked worktree.", "id": "TC-27", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:8e09b950"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T00:51:27.581Z", "seq": 41, "kind": "observation", "summary": "UPDATE (raised on THREE consecutive codex rounds in Slice 5): the eu7 no-run prohibition is still unenforceable, and scripts/agent-test.sh contains a bare 'cargo test --workspace' that WOULD execute eu7_real_corpus_ac. Slice 5 complied only by every implementer remembering to pass '-- --skip eu7_real_corpus_ac_validation' or to run targets individually. Original entry stands: the guard's shape needs a Steward/HITL decision because a naive #[ignore] creates the opposite (vacuous-green) hazard at Slice 40 where eu7 IS wanted.", "id": "TC-20", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:8e09b950"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T00:51:27.604Z", "seq": 42, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "DESIGN HARDENING OWED (FU-FIX3-3): the pending-redaction queue introduced in Slice 5 is a transactional outbox stored as ordinary operational_mutations rows (collection_name='erasure_pending_redaction'). Its 'a row is removed ONLY when the obligation is discharged' invariant is upheld by THREE separate correct call sites (retention sweep exemption, excise_collection_record refusal, the NotFound arm) rather than enforced structurally -- and it shipped with three ways to remove a row of which only one proved anything. Every known path is now closed, but NOTHING PREVENTS A FIFTH. Codex found a defect in this mechanism on each of rounds 1, 2 and 3. Structural options: give it its own table with no generic DELETE verb, or a trigger. Deliberately NOT done inside a fix round.", "id": "TC-28", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:8e09b950"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T00:51:27.627Z", "seq": 43, "kind": "observation", "summary": "RESIDUAL PATTERN (FU-FIX2-2): 'freeze the projection scanner, then wait on a DATABASE-derived idleness probe' is the shape behind a Slice-5 P2 where erase_source/excise_source could strand unqueued work and time out with EngineError::Scheduler after the full 30s LIFECYCLE_DRAIN_TIMEOUT_MS. purge and (now) the source-erasure path drain BEFORE freezing. run_rebuild is the LAST remaining instance that still freezes before its only drain -- likely benign (operator action, not a write-then-act sequence) but unaudited. Also logged: operator_cli::t_s34_dump_mutations_lock_held_exits_71 is FLAKY under cross-binary lock contention (it spawns its own lock holder); 25/25 in isolation, flaked once in a full-suite run. Touches no erasure path.", "id": "TC-29", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:7be20ec3"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T01:09:57.581Z", "seq": 44, "kind": "observation", "summary": "VACUOUS-GREEN HAZARD (found closing Slice 5): scripts/agent-lint-md.sh exits 0 SILENTLY from any linked worktree because node_modules/.bin/markdownlint-cli2 is absent there and the structural lint self-skips -- same failure class as TC-13 (eu7 corpus skip) and TC-25 (cfg-gated target compiles to zero tests). Running the real linter (npx --yes markdownlint-cli2) gives exit 1: 9 PRE-EXISTING MD025/MD001 issues in dev/research/personal-agent-database-market-2026-07-02.md, untouched since c366d6e0 (2026-07-02). Two implications: (a) the markdown gate is unreliable from every orchestration worktree, and (b) agent-lint-md.sh is likely RED in the main tree right now on that research file, independent of 0.8.20. Note also STATUS boards sit inside the config's dev/plans/runs/** ignore and are never linted.", "id": "TC-30", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open"}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T02:14:53.190Z", "seq": 45, "kind": "observation", "summary": "WRITE/READ PROVENANCE ASYMMETRY -- the 'NO SDK EXPOSURE' erasure gap, now measured. 0.8.20 makes provenance structurally MANDATORY on write (SourceId newtype), but PySearchHit.source_id is populated ONLY for graph-arm hits and is None for every two-arm (text/vector) hit -- documented at src/rust/crates/fathomdb-py/src/lib.rs:537-539. So a caller can be forced to supply provenance yet cannot read it back on an ordinary search hit. Measured consequence: BOTH remaining Python suite failures (test_option2_elps_pipeline::test_build_fathomdb_elps_path_uses_ingest_with_extractor and test_verify_embed_db::test_complete_embed_is_queryable) are caused by this -- _doc_id_of falls through the 'if sid:' branch because source_id is None, reaching int(sh.id) which has raised TypeError since 0.8.19 made SearchHit.id an IdSpace (C-2). ONE read-side fix (expose source_id on all arms) likely closes BOTH. Verified pre-existing: identical failures on origin/main f22e4947 in an isolated A/B clone; _doc_id_of is byte-identical on main.", "id": "TC-31", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:9c87d758"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T02:14:53.219Z", "seq": 46, "kind": "observation", "summary": "ERASURE GAP adjacent to R-20-E2 (found during fix-4, NOT fixed -- entity-identity design, out of slice scope): ingest_with_extractor dedupes entities within a batch by logical_id derived from (kind, name). Two documents naming the SAME entity collapse to ONE canonical row carrying the FIRST document's provenance. Consequence for erasure completeness: erasing the second document leaves that entity row behind, still attributed to the first document -- the row survives an erasure that should have removed the second document's contribution. Visible in the ELPS stub path (both docs yield 'TestEntity' => 1 node). Interacts with the multi-doc per-entity attribution contract added in 0.8.20: attribution is now required per entity, but dedup silently discards all but the first attribution.", "id": "TC-32", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:9c87d758"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T02:14:53.242Z", "seq": 47, "kind": "observation", "summary": "UPDATE -- GUARD SHIPPED, and the hazard was confirmed LIVE not hypothetical. src/python/tests/conftest.py now REFUSES to shell out to 'maturin develop' unless FATHOMDB_TESTS_ALLOW_REBUILD=1 is set (0.8.20 Slice 5, commit 265c54c0). During verification the probe showed the orchestration worktree's binding LACKS test-hooks, meaning the pre-guard code WOULD have rebuilt from that worktree into the shared .venv given an activated virtualenv. Downstream effect to know about: scripts/agent-test.sh's pytest step now FAILS LOUDLY instead of silently auto-rebuilding -- any isolated/throwaway verification environment must set FATHOMDB_TESTS_ALLOW_REBUILD=1 explicitly (the fresh-clone verification run did exactly this).", "id": "TC-27", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:265c54c0"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T03:11:17.565Z", "seq": 48, "kind": "observation", "summary": "RESOLVED and REWORKED in fix-6 -- SUPERSEDES the seq-47 update, which is now STALE (it said agent-test.sh 'FAILS LOUDLY' and told verification runners to export FATHOMDB_TESTS_ALLOW_REBUILD; both no longer accurate). codex flagged the fix-4 env-var guard as a P2: it turned a silent-rebuild hazard into a PERMANENTLY RED default pytest path (a clean 'pip install -e src/python[dev]' checkout raised at import time before collection). fix-6 restates TC-27 POSITIVELY: the policy is a pure function (src/python/tests/_test_hooks_gate.py) returning PROCEED/REBUILD/DEGRADED/CONTRADICTORY, and the load-bearing check is venv_belongs_to_source_tree() -- maturin develop may run ONLY when the venv prefix lies inside the repo root. The opt-in env var CANNOT override the ownership check, so the shared .venv is protected structurally rather than by an env var. agent-test.sh now sets the opt-in itself when it selected the in-tree .venv. Verified by execution in an isolated clone: default path collects (775 items, no raise); ownership check returns False for the shared venv and for a worktree venv and degrades instead of rebuilding; degraded mode skips EXACTLY the two hook-dependent tests with a visible banner (not a session-wide self-skip).", "id": "TC-27", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "resolved", "refs": ["git:d710721a"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T03:11:17.593Z", "seq": 49, "kind": "observation", "summary": "CORRECTION to TC-16's attribution, found by isolated-clone verification. The dead publish dry-run assertion that makes scripts/agent-test.sh exit 1 is NOT in test_pypi_publish_roundtrip.sh (that script passes cleanly). It is in scripts/tests/test_actionlint_fixture.sh, invoked at agent-test.sh line 63: 'FAIL publish-rust-t1-embedder-api dry-run branch is not cargo publish --dry-run -p'. Confirmed pre-existing -- both that script and .github/workflows/release.yml are byte-identical to origin/main. CONSEQUENCE THAT MATTERS: because the failure is at line 63 and agent-test.sh runs under 'set -euo pipefail', the script ABORTS THERE and NEVER REACHES the Rust or Python steps at all. So agent-test.sh's aggregate exit code tells you NOTHING about whether the test suites pass -- any agent reading it as a suite signal is reading a vacuous result. Run the steps individually until this is fixed.", "id": "TC-16", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "5", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:d710721a"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T18:15:47.518Z", "seq": 50, "kind": "observation", "summary": "TC-31 RESOLVED — source_id readable on every search-hit path (Py+TS), erase_source round-trip closed", "id": "TC-31", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "10", "status": "resolved", "refs": ["git:93a57b10", "plan:dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md"], "body": "Engine populated source_id at the 5 caller-reachable SearchHit sites (vector node, vector edge_fact, text/BM25F, pre-step-12 legacy text tiered, edge FTS) by adding one column to an existing SELECT/JOIN — no N+1 (there is no index on canonical_nodes(write_cursor)). Node hits carry canonical_nodes.source_id; edge hits carry canonical_edges.source_id; graph-arm semantics unchanged. rerank_passages left None deliberately: pure fn, no DB handle, hit never escapes to a caller — populating would fabricate provenance. Verified by execution in fresh clones: Python 787 passed/12 skipped, TS 186/186. codex §9 terminal PASS."}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T18:15:47.545Z", "seq": 51, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "TC-32 entity dedupe: HITL-ACCEPTED as-is; erasure guarantee must NOT be stated unconditionally to users", "id": "TC-32", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "10", "status": "accepted", "refs": ["git:93a57b10"], "body": "HITL ruling 2026-07-20: same-name entity dedupe collapsing onto one logical_id carrying the FIRST document's provenance is INTENTIONAL and ACCEPTED; annotate only, no behavior change, no multi-source-provenance API, no further analysis (explicitly time-boxed). Annotated at dedup_prepared_by_logical_id + derive_logical_id in engine/src/lib.rs and in the design doc. CARRY-FORWARD CAVEAT: the erasure guarantee 'erase every row owned by this source' does NOT hold for a co-named entity spanning documents, so user-facing docs and any GDPR/erasure claim must stay conditional until TC-32 is revisited."}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T18:15:47.568Z", "seq": 52, "kind": "open_question", "summary": "Node validity is INTEGER epoch while edge validity is ISO-8601 TEXT — temporal model now internally inconsistent", "id": "TC-33", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "10", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:93a57b10"], "body": "R-20-NV shipped canonical_nodes.valid_from/valid_until as INTEGER epoch seconds (sargable, and lets the instant be a BOUND :now parameter — the seam that makes validity deterministically testable). canonical_edges.t_valid/t_invalid remain ISO-8601 TEXT compared via datetime(), and the edge path still inlines datetime('now'). Edges deliberately untouched (unifying would re-litigate shipped semantics or force a data rewrite); divergence pinned by s22_edge_temporal_columns_are_untouched_text + r_20_nv_edge_temporal_filter_is_unchanged. HITL decision owed: accept the node/edge split long-term, or schedule a slice unifying edges onto INTEGER."}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T18:16:10.764Z", "seq": 53, "kind": "todo", "summary": "Node validity has NO write-side authoring verb — queryable but not settable from any SDK", "id": "TC-34", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "10", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:93a57b10"], "body": "R-20-NV as written specifies columns + read-side selector (valid_as_of) + crossed_boundary_since hook, but NOT a write verb, so Slice 10 shipped none. Consequence: a consumer can QUERY by validity window but cannot SET one — validity is not end-to-end usable from Python/TS/Rust. Tests author windows via direct SQL on a closed DB. This is the single most likely follow-up. HITL decision owed: is R-20-NV considered met without it, or does a write verb land before 0.8.20 publishes? Note a write verb GROWS the governed surface and would need to join the AC-079 delta."}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T18:16:10.792Z", "seq": 54, "kind": "observation", "summary": "napi #[napi(object)] Option::None OMITS the property — it is not null; ToNapiValue governs bare returns only", "id": "TC-35", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "10", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:9a6e4896"], "body": "MEASURED, after two wrong theory-driven type claims in one slice. napi-derive-backend 1.0.75 codegen/struct.rs obj_field_setters emits 'if field_.is_some() { obj.set(..) }' for use_nullable==false — None SKIPS the set, so the JS property is ABSENT (not null, not present-and-undefined). The else-branch emitting Null only exists under use_nullable=true, which appears ZERO times in fathomdb-napi. napi 2.16.17 ToNapiValue for Option<T> DOES map None->null, but #[napi(object)] fields never reach that impl (it governs bare returns / nested positions) — conflating the two is the trap. Convention in this repo: the public TS layer MANUFACTURES null via '?? null' (index.ts ceScore); a bare passthrough leaks undefined. Any future #[napi(object)] Option field must normalize with '?? null' or declare the field optional. STALE PROSE NOT FIXED: Rust doc comments on SearchHit.ce_score (fathomdb-napi/src/lib.rs:540-541) and PerHitExplain still say 'null' where native truth is 'absent'."}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T18:16:10.818Z", "seq": 55, "kind": "todo", "summary": "Published API docs still declare SearchHit id as int/number 'write_cursor' — stale since 0.8.19 C-2 IdSpace retype", "id": "TC-36", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "10", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:93a57b10"], "body": "docs/reference/python-api.md:380 and docs/reference/typescript-api.md:348 declare 'id: int' / 'id: number' with the comment 'canonical row write_cursor (interim identity carrier)'. Stale since 0.8.19 C-2 retyped SearchHit.id to IdSpace and stopped surfacing write_cursor to the SDKs entirely. Found during the TC-31 doc sweep and deliberately left out of scope (different staleness class). Small doc-only fix; no owning slice. Related: the same sweep found the docs stated source_id was graph-arm-only, which was the user-facing face of TC-31."}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T18:26:27.550Z", "seq": 56, "kind": "observation", "summary": "scripts/agent-lint-md.sh exits 0 VACUOUSLY in any worktree — self-skips both checks when node_modules/lychee absent", "id": "TC-37", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "10", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:9d387ede"], "body": "MEASURED: in a linked worktree there is no node_modules/.bin/markdownlint-cli2 and lychee is not on PATH, so agent-lint-md.sh takes its skip_notice branches (lines 12-15 and 34-41) for BOTH checks and exits 0 having linted NOTHING. An orchestrator/implementer that reports 'md-lint exit 0' from a worktree is reporting a vacuous green — this actually happened in Slice 10 and I passed it into a report before catching it. Same class as the known agent-test.sh TC-16 early-abort trap. Real result when run with the primary checkout's binary: plan-0.8.20.md 0 errors; STATUS-0.8.20.md is OUT OF SCOPE by config (.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc ignores dev/plans/runs/**); exit 1 comes from 9 PRE-EXISTING MD025/MD001 errors in dev/research/personal-agent-database-market-2026-07-02.md, a file untouched by this slice. FIX THE TOOLING: agent-lint-md.sh should exit non-zero (or loudly WARN) when it skips every check, so absence-of-tooling cannot masquerade as pass."}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T20:08:58.363Z", "seq": 57, "kind": "observation", "summary": "search is NOT one of the five read verbs — every ReadView visibility axis must be separately applied to Engine::search or it leaks", "id": "TC-38", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "15", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:ab790880"], "body": "MEASURED by codex §9 on Slice 15b. Slice 10 scoped ReadView to FIVE read verbs (read_get, read_get_many, read_list, read_list_filter, graph_neighbors). Engine::search is NOT among them: read_search_in_tx hydrates hits from search_index/vector rows using ONLY 'superseded_at IS NULL AND state = active', with no validity predicate. This was LATENT and unreachable while no SDK caller could author a validity window; TC-34 (Slice 15b) made authoring reachable from Py/TS and thereby turned it into a LIVE defect — an SDK-authored out-of-window node still came back from search while read_get/read_list correctly hid it. Note dev/design/record-lifecycle-protocol/api-surface.md:50 always specified ReadView on 'search' too, so the five-verb scope was a narrowing, not the contract. THE DURABLE LESSON, which outlives this fix: the five-read-verb ReadView plumbing and the search path are SEPARATE code paths, so ANY future visibility axis (a new relax-flag, a new lifecycle state, a new temporal dimension) added to ReadView will silently NOT apply to search unless separately wired. Slices 20 and 25 and any later release adding a visibility axis must check both. A search-based test CANNOT catch this class — assert on the read verbs AND on search."}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T20:08:58.387Z", "seq": 58, "kind": "todo", "summary": "AGENTS.md:25 mandates a dev/interfaces/* or ADR update in the same PR for any pub-Rust/SDK contract change — implementers keep missing it; DOC-INDEX rows are also incomplete", "id": "TC-39", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "15", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:ab790880"], "body": "codex §9 fired a [P2] on Slice 15b for shipping a public PreparedWrite::Node contract change plus new Py/TS write-item keys with NO dev/interfaces/* update. The rule is verbatim at AGENTS.md:25: 'Public surface is contract. Anything in dev/interfaces/ or pub Rust APIs is a contract; changes need an ADR or interface-doc update in the same PR.' It is a DISJUNCTION — an interface-doc update alone satisfies it; no ADR is separately required (searched AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, dev/interfaces/README.md, plan-0.8.20.md for an overriding convention; none found). TWO DURABLE ITEMS. (1) This is a recurring miss, not a one-off — the natural fix-the-tooling answer is a pre-commit/CI check that fails when a diff touches a pub engine type or a binding surface without touching dev/interfaces/*. Worth considering before more of 0.8.20's net-new surface lands unminuted. (2) MEASURED GAP: DOC-INDEX does NOT uniformly track dev/interfaces/* — only rust.md and cli.md had rows; python.md, typescript.md, wire.md and README.md had NONE. Slice 15b fix-1 added rows for python.md and typescript.md and refreshed rust.md, but DELIBERATELY left wire.md and README.md unrowed. DOC-INDEX's own header rule says a stale-or-missing row is an X3 gap that FAILS Slice 40 gate m — so wire.md and README.md are outstanding gate-m debt for 0.8.20 Slice 40 and are NOT attributable to any slice that touched them."}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T20:09:31.141Z", "seq": 59, "kind": "consideration", "summary": "R-20-PR registry shape: plan's roles:{filterable,rankable,searchable} as three booleans CANNOT express the ratified C-1 contract — must be a SET of typed roles", "id": "TC-40", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "15", "status": "open", "refs": ["plan:dev/design/record-lifecycle-protocol/OPP-12-C1-converged-contract.md"], "body": "ORCHESTRATOR DESIGN FINDING, code-grounded, for whoever builds R-20-PR/R-20-EAV. plan-0.8.20.md §1/§3 summarises the registry as 'ProjectionSpec {name, roles: {filterable, rankable, searchable}}'. Read as three booleans that is UNBUILDABLE against the RATIFIED C-1 contract, for three independent reasons. (1) C-1 Q4's whole tiering mechanism is 'declare the cheap roles now, ADD the vector role at promote(), drop on reject' — that REQUIRES searchable->FTS and searchable->vector to be SEPARATELY declarable; one 'searchable' boolean cannot express it. (2) The staleness contract (projection-registry-and-async-embed.md §3) splits them by guarantee: filterable + searchable->FTS are SAME-TRANSACTION, searchable->vector is ASYNC carrying dense_readiness. (3) Slice 20 (R-20-DR, dense_readiness + atomic flip) attaches ONLY to searchable->vector — a boolean gives Slice 20 nothing to attach to. C-1 Q1 also fixes the wire shape: Memex ALREADY LANDED 'AttributeSpec.index: Set[ProjectionRole]' inert at memex 95ed450, so the engine must accept a SET or the ratified cross-repo seam does not connect. Recommended: enum ProjectionRole { Filterable, SearchableFts{tokenizer:Option<String>}, SearchableVector{embedder:Option<String>}, Rankable } with roles as a set; absent tokenizer/embedder => engine default (C-1 'Tokenizer/embedder defaults' section). This matters beyond Slice 15 because Slice 30's RUBRIC-H7 can-i-deploy gate mechanically checks as-built code against OPP-12-C1-converged-contract.md — a three-boolean shape would FAIL Slice 30 and HOLD the breaking pair."}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T20:09:31.164Z", "seq": 60, "kind": "consideration", "summary": "R-20-PR: 'filterable' has TWO incompatible backends — vec0 pre-KNN filtering cannot be extended without a full re-embed; ADR-0.8.11 D3 forbids the post-KNN fallback", "id": "TC-41", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "15", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:29eba153"], "body": "ORCHESTRATOR DESIGN FINDING, code-grounded. The 'filterable' role resolves to storage through TWO different, incompatible engines today. (A) SEARCH/vector backend: vector_filter_clause (engine lib.rs:8685-8710) is a HARDCODED four-column map onto vec0 metadata columns {source_type, kind, created_at, status}. vec0 is a virtual table with NO ALTER, so adding a fifth filterable field on the pre-KNN path means DROP+RECREATE vector_default and RE-EMBED THE WHOLE CORPUS — per registration. Unacceptable. (B) read.list backend: Predicate::to_sql_clause emits json_extract(body, path) gated by the COMPILE-TIME const PREDICATE_PATH_ALLOWLIST (lib.rs:1808) of exactly six paths. The obvious escape — join EAV after the KNN — is EXPLICITLY FORBIDDEN by ADR-0.8.11 D3 no-demotion, which is enforced in the type system: a Json filter term is TYPE-REJECTED on the vector backend (lib.rs:2038-2044). So all three options are constrained and the tension is REAL, not incidental. NOTE the C-1 contract describes filterable as a 'pre-KNN constraint' (projection-registry-and-async-embed.md §2), i.e. backend (A) — the expensive one. ORCHESTRATOR RECOMMENDATION (NOT ratified, owed to the HITL): scope filterable to backend (B) for 0.8.20 — the registry supersedes the compile-time PREDICATE_PATH_ALLOWLIST with a runtime-registered set, and the vec0 metadata filter set stays FROZEN at its four columns. This is a DEVIATION from the contract's pre-KNN wording and must be ratified, not assumed, because Slice 30's H7 can-i-deploy gate checks as-built against that contract."}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T20:09:58.062Z", "seq": 61, "kind": "open_question", "summary": "TC-33 migration story worked out: node/edge temporal split CONFIRMS the INTEGER recommendation; blast radius is small in schema but crosses an external BYO-LLM wire format", "id": "TC-33", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "15", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:29eba153"], "body": "ORCHESTRATOR DESIGN WORK on the HITL-directed harmonisation. CONCLUSION: converge on INTEGER epoch seconds, i.e. the Steward recommendation STANDS — no escalation of the direction call is required on the merits. SCHEMA BLAST RADIUS IS SMALLER THAN FEARED: exactly ONE table, TWO columns (canonical_edges.t_valid/t_invalid, TEXT, added by step 14), and ZERO indexes reference either column, so no index rebuild is forced. BUT the real cost is NOT the schema, it is FIVE surfaces: (1) 8 SQL predicate sites that today inline datetime('now') (engine lib.rs:7806, 9553, 9685, 10074, 10112, 10903, 10970, 4539) must switch to a BOUND :now — which is a strict improvement, it is what makes edge validity deterministically testable the way node validity already is; (2) PreparedWrite::Edge t_valid/t_invalid Option<String> -> Option<i64>, a breaking binding-coordination change; (3) ConsolidateCandidateEdge AND its harness JSON contract (lib.rs:4405-4406) which is an EXTERNAL BYO-LLM wire format; (4) temporal_fallback is derived by STRING EQUALITY comparing t_valid against warnings[].substituted_t_valid (lib.rs:4302-4305, 4123-4141) and BREAKS SILENTLY under a type change — this is the sharpest edge and needs its own RED test; (5) two tests that deliberately PIN the TEXT representation (s22_edge_temporal_columns_are_untouched_text in schema/tests/step22_migration.rs:211, r_20_nv_edge_temporal_filter_is_unchanged in engine/tests/slice10_node_validity.rs:624) must be rewritten, not deleted — they were the drift guard and must be replaced by an INTEGER equivalent. LAYERING CALL OWED TO THE HITL: storage and the governed SDK surface should both become INTEGER, but the BYO-LLM EXTRACTOR JSON should KEEP ISO-8601 with engine-side normalisation on ingest — an LLM cannot reliably emit epoch seconds, and that boundary already has a normalisation layer. This is a layering boundary, not splitting the difference on the representation; flagging it explicitly because it is adjacent enough to the direction call that the HITL should confirm it. MIGRATION MECHANICS: SQLite has no ALTER COLUMN TYPE, and the pinning test asserts declared type == TEXT, so true harmonisation requires a 12-step TABLE REBUILD of canonical_edges (create-new / copy-with-conversion / drop / rename), not an UPDATE in place. HAZARD: strftime('%s', x) returns NULL on an unparseable value, and a NULL t_invalid READS AS 'still valid' — so a junk value silently RESURRECTS an invalidated edge. That must be a RED test and a migration-time hard failure, never a silent NULL. SCHEDULING NOTE: this needs schema step 24 (23 being the EAV/registry step), making FOUR migrations in 0.8.20. I-6 'one migration per release' is GUIDANCE, not enforced — there is no test counting steps per release, and 0.8.20 already ships two (21 and 22), which dev/plans/0.8.6-0.8.16-PROGRAM-SEQUENCING.md:417 explicitly blesses. So four is precedented but should be surfaced, not slipped through."}
{"ts": "2026-07-20T21:35:56.822Z", "seq": 62, "kind": "observation", "summary": "GATE-INTEGRITY: R-20-NV was closed at Slice 10 while one of its three Slice-0-named RED tests (valid_as_of_binds_now_once) was NEVER WRITTEN — and that exact property then regressed", "id": "TC-42", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "15", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:a8087dfb"], "body": "MEASURED, and the failure it predicted actually happened. dev/design/0.8.20-slice0-erasure-design.md:308 names THREE falsifiable RED tests for R-20-NV: validity_window_matrix, valid_as_of_binds_now_once, crossed_boundary_since_hook. A grep across src/ finds ZERO hits for valid_as_of_binds_now_once — it exists ONLY in that design table. R-20-NV was nevertheless declared CLOSED at Slice 10. The property it was supposed to pin (':now' binds ONCE PER QUERY, not per row/arm — a per-arm bind makes results non-deterministic across a validity boundary) then REGRESSED in Slice 15b fix-2, where the graph arm re-read current_epoch_seconds independently of the instant already resolved for the text/vector arms. codex §9 caught it; the never-written test would have caught it first. TWO DURABLE CONSEQUENCES. (1) TRACEABILITY IS UNENFORCED: a Slice-0 design can name a RED test, the slice can close without writing it, and nothing detects the gap — the acceptance table is prose, not a gate. Slice 40's DoD should mechanically check that every test NAMED in the Slice-0 acceptance tables actually EXISTS in src/, for every requirement, not just this one. Other named-but-unverified tests may exist across R-20-RV/PR/EAV/DR/SUR — this was found by accident, so assume it is not the only one. (2) NAMING: the property is now pinned for the first time by default_view_search_reads_the_clock_once_per_query (Slice 15b fix-3), which does NOT match the design's name. Either rename it or cross-reference it, or a traceability sweep will still report R-20-NV as missing a test."}
{"ts": "2026-07-21T07:15:33.052Z", "seq": 63, "kind": "question", "summary": "Extractor timestamp rejection uses InvalidArgument while the parallel consolidation path uses Consolidator (after TC-33 fix-3) — should the extractor leaf be Extractor?", "area": "fathomdb-engine/error-taxonomy", "epistemic": "proposed", "id": "TC-43", "owner": "hitl", "priority": "p3", "status": "open", "refs": ["file:src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/src/lib.rs:1950", "file:src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/src/lib.rs:4995", "file:src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine/tests/tc33_edge_temporal_epoch.rs"], "body": "TC-33 fix-3 remaps a malformed/non-string consolidation-verdict t_invalid from EngineError::InvalidArgument to EngineError::Consolidator, because consolidation is a BYO-LLM PROVIDER boundary whose documented leaf is Consolidator (matching the two sibling failure modes: missing key, null/unparseable-to-None). But normalize_extractor_timestamp is ALSO called on the EXTRACTOR ingest boundary (lib.rs:4665-4684), which is likewise a BYO-LLM provider boundary and has its OWN leaf EngineError::Extractor at the enum. One could argue a malformed EXTRACTOR timestamp is symmetrically a provider fault (Extractor), not a user InvalidArgument. This is a broader taxonomy call the HITL/steward should weigh. NOT resolved here and deliberately OUT OF SCOPE for fix-3: the 15c GREEN tests tc33_edge_temporal_epoch.rs PIN that the extractor rejection is InvalidArgument{msg} and that msg names the offending field (t_valid/t_invalid); changing the extractor leaf would break those pinned tests. Deferred."}
{"ts": "2026-07-21T07:57:03.726Z", "seq": 64, "kind": "observation", "summary": "strftime('%s',?) used as an ISO-8601 validator but SQLite's date parser is MORE LENIENT than the declared contract — codex found 3 leniencies across 2 rounds; safety-critical no-NULL property intact (wrong-instant, not resurrection)", "area": "fathomdb-engine/tc33-timestamp-normalisation", "id": "TC-44", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:7ca664f7", "plan:dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md"], "body": "TC-33 normalises edge timestamps by delegating to SQLite strftime/date fns, leakier than strict ISO-8601: (1) too-large epochs [fixed fix-1]; (2) pre-year-0000 epochs render non-NULL '-001-12-31T23:59:59Z' so reject_unrenderable_edge_epoch (engine:1892-1894) passes them despite outside declared 0000..=9999; (3) bare numeric strings '0'/'2451545.0' treated as JULIAN-DAY -> epoch not NULL (engine:1824-1827), so non-ISO wire input stored as unrelated instant. SAFETY: none yield NULL t_invalid, so HITL Note1/P3 'no resurrection via NULL' INTACT — wrong-instant not fail-open. Root fix: strict ISO-8601 shape gate (regex, no dep) before SQLite, plus numeric MIN/MAX range check on governed i64 path. HALTED at fix-N bound (5th codex round) pending HITL/steward: consolidated root-cause fix vs accept-as-pre-1.0. Distinct from TC-43."}
{"ts": "2026-07-21T07:57:03.753Z", "seq": 65, "kind": "observation", "summary": "PRE-EXISTING (not TC-33): fix-32 supersession writes projection terminals with state='superseded' but the terminal-table CHECK allows only ('failed','up_to_date') and the writer uses INSERT OR IGNORE — terminals SILENTLY DROPPED, supersession path can stall the projection cursor", "area": "fathomdb-engine/projection-cursor", "id": "TC-45", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:7ca664f7"], "body": "record_projection_terminal (INSERT OR IGNORE) called engine:14867/:14890 with state='superseded' by fix-32 supersession. _fathomdb_projection_terminal (schema step7) CHECK(state IN ('failed','up_to_date')). INSERT OR IGNORE skips CHECK-violating rows silently, so the terminal never persists; advance_projection_cursor can stall at a superseded cursor with no terminal — same class TC-33 fix-4 fixed for migration. PRE-EXISTING (fix-32, prior slice), NOT introduced by TC-33, left untouched (out of scope). Surfaced by the fix-4 implementer while proving the 'up_to_date' token. Needs its own fix; likely 'up_to_date' at both sites."}
{"ts": "2026-07-21T08:26:06.514Z", "seq": 66, "kind": "observation", "summary": "TC-44 RESOLVED by TC-33 fix-5: strict ISO-8601 shape gate before strftime (rejects Julian-day/bare-numeric wire input) + numeric MIN/MAX epoch range check replacing renderability (rejects pre-year-0000 governed i64) — whole strftime-leniency class closed", "area": "fathomdb-engine/tc33-timestamp-normalisation", "id": "TC-44", "status": "resolved", "refs": ["seq:64", "git:895b9df0", "plan:dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md"], "body": "fix-5 (GREEN 895b9df0): (a) is_iso8601_shape() hand-rolled ASCII-byte shape gate (YYYY-MM-DD[(T|space)HH:MM:SS[.fff][Z|+-HH:MM|+-HHMM]]) runs BEFORE strftime in iso8601_to_epoch_seconds; '2451545.0' (Julian day) and '0' now return None -> existing hard-reject fires, ZERO rows written (raw canonical_edges count asserted). NO new dependency: no chrono/time in workspace, regex only transitive via jsonschema; hand-rolled. Shape-valid-but-impossible dates (2025-13-45) still None via strftime. (b) reject_unrenderable_edge_epoch() now tests numeric [MIN_RENDERABLE_EPOCH=-62167219200, MAX_RENDERABLE_EPOCH=253402300799] bounds directly, not renderability; pre-year-0000 epoch -62167219201 (renders NON-NULL -001-12-31T23:59:59Z) now rejected. Consts verified vs SQLite = 0000-01-01T00:00:00Z .. 9999-12-31T23:59:59Z exactly. No NULL path opened; safety-critical no-resurrection property intact. RED 3-fail/5-pass then GREEN 8/8 in tc33_fix5_iso8601_shape_gate.rs; full engine+schema suite 588 passed/0 failed/13 ignored. Positive controls (Z, +00:00, date-only, space-separated) still accepted at correct epoch."}
{"ts": "2026-07-21T13:07:58.417Z", "seq": 67, "kind": "question", "summary": "Slice 15e gated vec0 filterable reshape: the ProjectionDestructive drop-list acknowledgement channel is defined for REMOVING a filterable but UNDEFINED for ADDING a net-new filterable that (per decision-4) still wipes all embeddings — needs a total ack channel; Steward direction owed", "area": "fathomdb-engine/projection-registry-vec0-reshape", "id": "TC-46", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:282a03f7", "plan:dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md"], "body": "15e must reshape vec0 to carry a registry-declared filterable attribute as a PRE-KNN metadata column (decision 2, ADR D3). Decision 4: the reshape does NOT preserve embedding blobs, so ANY filterable-set change (ADD or REMOVE) is destructive to the vector index and must be gated. Slice 15d's gate is ProjectionDestructive + an explicit drop-list — which names a projection to REMOVE. When ADDING a net-new filterable attribute there is nothing to put in the drop-list, yet the ADD still triggers a full embedding wipe, so it must be acknowledged. The drop-list channel is therefore undefined for the ADD case. Implementer recommendation (sound): a typed reshape-acknowledgement parameter on configure_projections (e.g. allow_reshape / a ReshapeAck), the only channel total across ADD and REMOVE — a GOVERNED-SURFACE addition needing sign-off. Also verified: vec0 rejects quoted column identifiers (attr cols need byte-safe encoding e.g. attr_<hex-of-utf8>); vec0 rejects partial-column INSERT (all ~6 INSERT sites must bind every attr col — inherently multi-site); the async projection worker carries no node body (write-path population is net-new); run_pin_and_requantize_pass (engine:8973) is a SAME-SHAPE re-quantize that must PRESERVE attr cols — decision-4 no-preservation applies ONLY to the filterable-set-change reshape, NOT to same-shape re-quantize (conflating would wipe attr values on every mean-pin). Proposed decomposition 15e-1 (shape param + gated reshape + pre-KNN compile, attr cols ship '' sentinel, population deferred — mirrors how Slice 10 shipped status) / 15e-2 (write-path + rebuild_vec0 population) / 15e-3 (governed filter-predicate + reshape-ack across Py/TS). 15e-1 is BLOCKED on this ack-channel decision."}
{"ts": "2026-07-21T23:22:53.452Z", "seq": 68, "kind": "decision", "summary": "RESOLVED — HITL chose Option 1: NON-DESTRUCTIVE vec0 reshape. Ack-channel gap DISSOLVES (no new governed surface). NO-DATA-MIGRATION is scoped to the cross-version schema step, NOT a runtime reshape of a live DB. Follow migrate_vector_partition_pack1_to_pack2 (engine:13492).", "id": "TC-46", "status": "resolved", "refs": ["git:cf2f155c"], "body": "The premise 'a reshape must wipe embeddings' was over-applying NO-DATA-MIGRATION (which governs the 0.8.9->0.8.20 cross-version schema step) to a RUNTIME reconfiguration. A non-destructive re-insert keeps old+new rows aligned/coherent/queryable, and the tree already ships exactly that op (pack1->pack2). So a filterable ADD reshape is NON-destructive => no acknowledgement parameter needed, no ProjectionDestructive for the ADD case; idempotent re-registration is a no-op; a shape change re-inserts non-destructively. FOUR load-bearing conditions (all must hold or results go SILENTLY wrong): (1) list rowid EXPLICITLY in the re-insert (rowid==write_cursor, engine:7086/:10005; auto-assign decouples embedding from node); (2) new attr column is plain metadata OR partition key, NEVER a vec0 aux/'+' column (aux hard-errors filtered KNN, engine:13460-13464 — this is what makes pre-KNN D3 work); (3) back-fill old rows with '' sentinel (vec0 TEXT metadata NOT-NULL-able, engine:7091) so they fail-to-match cleanly; (4) copy embedding_bin VERBATIM via vec_bit(...), do NOT re-quantize (re-deriving bits from raw embedding leaves old rows un-centered vs new mean-centered => incomparable Hamming, silent recall corruption; anti-pattern at engine:13584-13599). run_pin_and_requantize_pass is a separate same-shape op — leave alone. Scope: filterable already works via 15d EAV (canonical_attributes); 15e adds ONLY pre-KNN vector-path routing (D3, no post-KNN json_extract demotion)."}
{"ts": "2026-07-22T22:50:10.094Z", "seq": 69, "kind": "question", "summary": "SURVIVING TEMPORAL P2 (calendar-rollover sub-class of TC-44): strftime silently ROLLS OVER a shape-valid impossible DAY (2025-02-30 -> 2025-03-02) instead of NULL; the is_iso8601_shape gate (fix-5) checks format, not calendar validity. ESCALATED per the ratified sixth-round rule (no reflexive temporal fix).", "area": "fathomdb-engine/tc33-timestamp-normalisation", "id": "TC-47", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:0b47349c", "seq:64"], "body": "Verified vs SQLite: strftime('%s','2025-02-30T00:00:00Z') -> epoch that round-trips to 2025-03-02; '2025-04-31'->'2025-05-01'; but '2025-13-01' (month 13) -> NULL. So SQLite rejects impossible MONTHS but silently rolls over impossible DAYS. TC-44's fix-5 shape gate accepts 2025-02-30 (well-formatted YYYY-MM-DD), so a BYO-LLM edge timestamp of Feb 30 stores a DIFFERENT instant than the provider supplied — bypassing the hard-reject contract. SAFETY: the stored value is a valid instant, not NULL, so the 'no resurrection via NULL' property (HITL Note 1 / P3) HOLDS — this is wrong-instant, not fail-open. Clean complete fix available: a CALENDAR ROUND-TRIP (strftime the epoch back to a date and compare the date component to the input; reject on mismatch) closes the whole rollover sub-class, not another partial patch. Options for HITL/steward: (a) calendar round-trip validation [recommended]; (b) accept as documented pre-1.0 (safety intact); (c) rethink strftime-as-validator (no chrono/time in workspace by design). Per the ratified rule, this is a design call, not a reflexive fix. Distinct, non-temporal same-round finding: TS configure_projections wrapper skips validateFfiString (lone UTF-16 surrogate -> U+FFFD persists) — bounded AC-068 gate fix, folded into the closeout."}
{"ts": "2026-07-24T12:56:20.969Z", "seq": 70, "kind": "todo", "summary": "Status-board currency enforcement — design dev/design/status-board-currency-enforcement.md; first effort scope = items 1-3 (seam-ownership contract + preflight land-gate + CI drift detector); commission an orchestrator via the next steward", "detail": "HITL-directed 2026-07-24. Motivation: the 0.8.20 STATUS board lied for four days after Slices 10/15 landed (said Slice 15 PARTIAL/unlanded) because the LANDED board state falls in an ownership seam between the orchestrator (owns in-flight board) and the steward (does the land, reconciles master+ledger, skipped the board). Fix the mechanism, not the actor. FIRST EFFORT = items 1-3 of the design: (1) contract line assigning the steward the LANDED row + next-slice pointer, updated IN the landing merge (edit .claude/agents/steward.md + orchestration.md section 12.5); (2) board-currency GATE in scripts/preflight.sh --landing that refuses a land leaving STATUS-0.8.z.md stale, RED-first test; (3) CI drift detector on main, red when board disagrees with git ancestry. PLACEMENT ANSWER (HITL asked): NOT precommit (board legitimately reads in-flight during a build => false positives; precommit can only check well-formedness). Prevent at land-time in preflight (mandatory land gate, blocks before stale board reaches main); DETECT via CI on main (non-bypassable backstop for what slips preflight or predates it); prepush is optional shift-left, not a substitute (--no-verify-able, local-config-dependent). Item 4 (machine-derived LANDED table from git ancestry) is LATER, only if drift persists despite 1-3; when built its check is 'regenerate + assert no diff' in the same two homes (preflight + CI). Applies to EVERY release board + the master, not just 0.8.20. TDD-able, docs+scripts+one CI job, no engine code. Aligns with guardrail-failures-fix-tooling-not-people. Proposed to the next steward in STEWARD-SESSION-HANDOFF-2026-07-24-A.md (commission alongside Slice 20 or as its own micro-effort).", "owner": "next-steward-to-commission-orchestrator", "status": "OPEN"}
