{"ts": "2026-07-25T18:52:35.955Z", "seq": 76, "kind": "todo", "summary": "POST-0.8.20 SLICE OWED — dev/design/** status: frontmatter backfill is DELIBERATELY PROVISIONAL and must be remediated in its own slice. DOC-HYGIENE-2 T2c introduces a frontmatter gate over dev/design/** (108 docs, only 23 carry status: today) and backfills the remaining 85. Per HITL 2026-07-25 the backfill DEFAULTS TO status: UNREVIEWED for anything not confidently classifiable, because a doc wrongly marked ACTIVE is WORSE than one with no marker - a falsely-current design doc is exactly the failure mode this program keeps re-hitting (see the 0.6.0-era dev/architecture.md still frontmattered target_release 0.6.0 / locked, and README.md still calling FathomDB a rewrite-in-progress). Only docs with clear evidence - an existing supersession banner, a closed-release tie - get ACTIVE or SUPERSEDED at backfill time. OWED: a dedicated post-0.8.20 slice that does the real classification, converting UNREVIEWED to a truthful status per doc. Until then the gate proves PRESENCE of a status field, never its TRUTH.", "decider": "hitl", "id": "TC-50", "status": "open", "refs": ["plan:dev/plans/0.8.6-0.8.16-PROGRAM-SEQUENCING.md", "seq:106"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-25T22:22:46.422Z", "seq": 77, "kind": "todo", "summary": "DOC-HYGIENE-3 BACKLOG (from DOC-HYGIENE-2 escalations; placed by HITL O3 into the Slice 30 -> Slice 40 window). Four carried items, none blocking Slice 20. (a) ~55 FURTHER ROTTEN LINE-ANCHORS in plan-0.8.20.md in shapes T1d's narrow ANCHOR_RE does not match - the section 0.1 ROOT-CAUSE table driving Slice 5 is measurably wrong (offsets +4680, +2502, -8160); symbols exist so the findings hold, but nobody can navigate by the numbers. Widening ANCHOR_RE is one line but reddens ~80 sites, so it needs its own budget. (b) LEDGER VOCABULARY CONTRADICTS ITS OWN README: TC-7 was ruled but carries no kind=decision so it is invisible to the T2b ruling registry; kind is declared immutable yet TC-8 walks consideration->decision; the declared terminal vocabulary matches ZERO real entries; todos seq-24 rules on TC-11 with id=null. Adjudicating means appending to a ledger, so it needs a decision first. (c) UNKNOWN vs UNREVIEWED are near-synonyms across two vocabularies introduced by T2c - a single vocabulary is owed once TC-50 drains. (d) 46 pre-existing free-form dev/design status values (locked, decision-ready, ...) grandfathered behind a frozen LEGACY_RE + one-way budget ratchet; rewriting them IS the TC-50 classification and must not be done piecemeal.", "decider": "steward", "id": "TC-51", "status": "open", "refs": ["seq:106", "git:986dd912"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-25T22:22:46.450Z", "seq": 78, "kind": "todo", "summary": "ALLOWLIST _comment PROSE CONTRADICTS THE PRE-SIGN, and correcting it TRIPS THE T1e PIN. src/conformance/governed-surface-allowlist.json's _comment still reads 'AWAITING HITL SIGN-OFF, NOT SIGNED' for slices 5d/10b/15b/15d, which is false as of 427d2712 (master F-34 - the HITL pre-signed the accumulated delta). The T1e pin binds to that file's EXACT CONTENT (hash 93ef3a86...), so a prose-only correction re-opens the gate and requires a deliberate re-pin under HITL sign-off - it is NOT a docs fix. DELIBERATELY DEFERRED to the batched governed-surface decision at the Slice 30 -> Slice 40 boundary, where a re-pin happens anyway (Slice 20 is expected to trip the pin) and where AC-079 mints. Until then the TRUTH is carried by the generated status-unblocks view (STATUS section 1), the corrected section 4 row 7, plan section 11's 2026-07-25 rulings block, and the Slice-20 commission brief - so no implementer should be misled. DO NOT hand-edit the allowlist prose before that decision.", "decider": "steward", "id": "TC-52", "status": "open", "refs": ["seq:106", "git:7b6a67ba"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-25T22:23:55.824Z", "seq": 79, "kind": "todo", "summary": "LEDGERWRITE HAS NO GUARD AGAINST SHELL-SUBSTITUTION CORRUPTION AT WRITE TIME, and it has now silently mangled two entries. seq-95/96 (a backtick substitution dropped one word) and seq-108/109 (an exit-status variable expanded to 0 and a command substitution expanded to empty, its stderr showing \"basename: missing operand\"). Both were caught only because a human/Steward re-read the written record; a malformed summary is otherwise indistinguishable from an intended one, and the ledger is APPEND-ONLY so the damage can only be corrected by a follow-up entry, never repaired in place. Per the standing rule that guardrail failures are fixed in TOOLING and not by telling people to be careful: ledgerwrite should refuse-or-warn when a --summary contains characters that indicate an unintended expansion already happened or is likely (a lone $ , an empty $() residue, backticks), and/or dev/steward/README.md should mandate single-quoted summaries with a worked example. Note the failure is at the CALLER (the shell expands before ledgerwrite ever sees the string), so a pure in-tool check can only catch residue, not intent - the README half of the fix is load-bearing. Placed in the DOC-HYGIENE-3 window alongside TC-51.", "decider": "steward", "id": "TC-53", "status": "open", "refs": ["seq:109", "seq:96"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T00:23:51.952Z", "seq": 80, "kind": "observation", "summary": "RESOLVED BY HITL 2026-07-25: .markdownlint-cli2.jsonc ignoring .claude/** is INTENTIONAL and stays. A subagent flagged it as a coverage gap while adding the /decisions command, on the grounds that .claude/commands/*.md ARE git-tracked while the config comment claimed they were gitignored. The comment was factually wrong; the IGNORE is correct. Reason recorded in the config: these files are prompt text read by an agent, not published prose, so markdownlint structural rules police a shape that carries no meaning and a lint failure would block a command edit for style. The stale justification has been corrected in place so no future agent removes the ignore on the strength of a false premise. NO gate change; NO coverage added. Do not re-open.", "decider": "hitl", "id": "TC-54", "status": "closed", "refs": ["file:.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T00:33:28.289Z", "seq": 81, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "BLOCKER FOR SLICES 20c/25/30 - the governed-surface pin gate makes 'record the delta as a proposal AND land' mechanically impossible. plan-0.8.20.md section 11 ruling 2 (HITL 2026-07-25) says new governed surface at 20/25/30 is recorded as a proposal, the branch stays green, and the accumulated delta goes to the HITL once at the Slice 30 to Slice 40 boundary. But check-governed-surface-pin.sh compares a sha256 AND git-blob-sha1 over the RAW BYTES of src/conformance/governed-surface-allowlist.json, so ANY diff - including the _comment prose edit that IS the proposal convention used by Slices 5d/10b/15b/15d - hard-fails the gate. preflight.sh --landing invokes that gate as a HARD fail, so the slice cannot land. The gate header asserts that tripping it is 'precisely what lets Slices 20/25/30 proceed WITHOUT stopping for a per-slice sign-off'; as wired that claim is FALSE - tripping it blocks the land. Slice 20 was split so part b (dense_readiness) adds ZERO governed commands and lands clean; part c (flush_embeddings) is held. Options: (a) FIX THE TOOLING - give the pin a pending_delta block so the gate passes iff allowlist == pinned union pending_delta, loudly reporting the unsigned set, preserving every guarantee incl. the locked recovery_denylist; (b) fresh HITL sign-off plus re-pin per slice, which contradicts the batching ruling; (c) reclassify. Recommend (a).", "area": "release/0.8.20/governed-surface", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-54", "priority": "p1", "status": "open", "refs": ["plan:dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md", "file:scripts/check-governed-surface-pin.sh", "file:scripts/preflight.sh"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T00:33:28.317Z", "seq": 82, "kind": "open-question", "summary": "UNRESOLVED CLASSIFICATION that decides whether Slice 20c trips the surface pin at all - is flush_embeddings a governed APPLICATION COMMAND or an INSTRUMENTATION/CONTROL method? plan-0.8.20.md section 11 ruling 2 states it 'reads as a net-new command' and expects the pin to trip. But dev/design/record-lifecycle-protocol/api-surface.md C4 says the opposite - reuse the shipped drain(timeout_ms) barrier INSTEAD of adding flush_embeddings - and projection-registry-and-async-embed.md section 4 repeats it. Both design docs are status UNREVIEWED, so the plan is the contract and the plan wins; recorded because the docs will otherwise keep misleading readers, and TC-50 owes them a classification. Load-bearing mechanical detail: the shipped drain is NOT in the JSON allowlist - it sits in the _INSTRUMENTATION exclusion set hardcoded in BOTH src/python/tests/test_surface.py and src/ts/tests/surface.test.ts ('observability, NOT application commands'). So classifying flush_embeddings as instrumentation would leave the pinned JSON untouched and let 20c land clean, while classifying it as a command forces an allowlist edit and hits TC-54. That makes the classification a real design question with a large mechanical consequence - it must NOT be settled by whichever answer is easier to land. Design rider that survives either way: drain is a BARRIER (wait-for-idle), flush must also be a TRIGGER, so deferred/backfill rows have to be enqueued on the same projection runtime drain waits on.", "area": "release/0.8.20/api-surface", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-55", "priority": "p1", "status": "open", "refs": ["plan:dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md", "file:dev/design/record-lifecycle-protocol/api-surface.md", "tc:TC-54"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T00:33:28.342Z", "seq": 83, "kind": "observation", "summary": "Slice 20 fix-1 CLOSED A SHIPPED Engine::drain DEFECT as a side effect - a behaviour change on an already-published surface that the 0.8.20 changelog and the Slice 40 publish narrative must reflect. The pending-work probe (database_has_pending_projection_work, which backs ProjectionRuntime::wait_for_idle and therefore the public Engine::drain) omitted the scheduler's JOIN against _fathomdb_vector_kinds on the edge arm, while next_pending_projection_jobs carried it. Consequence: a live canonical edge WITH A BODY whose edge_fact kind is not registered counts as outstanding work that the scheduler will never schedule, so it never gains a projection terminal - phantom-pending forever, and drain burns its whole timeout and returns Err(Scheduler). Confirmed PRE-EXISTING empirically, not by inspection: a detached checkout at baseline 9db32765 using only baseline surfaces returned BASELINE-PROBE drain result = Err(Scheduler) after the full timeout, with zero Slice-20 code in the build. Found because dense_readiness derives from that same predicate, which is what surfaced it. Fix shares ONE definition (pending_edge_projection_from_where) between probe and scheduler so they cannot drift again. Deliberate preserved asymmetry: the scheduler filters write_cursor > cursor and the probe does not, per the Slice-15 G11 fix-1 note, so the probe still sees edge bodies left un-projected below the watermark after a mid-flight close.", "area": "engine/projection-runtime", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-56", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:2b1f62d0", "slice:0.8.20-20b-fix-1"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T00:33:28.366Z", "seq": 84, "kind": "observation", "summary": "PRE-EXISTING ENGINE DEFECT, reproducible at baseline, no owning plan - a loop of GOVERNED writes racing the async projection worker intermittently returns EngineError::Storage. Shape: PreparedWrite::Node with logical_id Some(..) in a loop while the projection worker runs; reproduced at baseline 9db32765 with ZERO Slice-20 code in the build, failing 7 of 8 runs (e.g. 'governed write 4 failed: Storage'). NEVER reproducible with logical_id None, which points at the supersession read-then-write upgrade on a deferred transaction - most likely SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT, which busy_timeout cannot retry. This matters beyond a flaky test: governed writes are exactly the Phase-2 surface 0.8.20 publishes, so a host doing bulk governed ingest can hit it. Slice 20 deliberately routed around it (its concurrency test uses anonymous writes, documented in a comment) rather than dragging an unrelated red into R-20-DR. Needs placement - candidate for Slice 40 triage or a 0.8.21 fixup.", "area": "engine/write-path", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-57", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:9db32765", "slice:0.8.20-20b"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T00:33:28.389Z", "seq": 85, "kind": "observation", "summary": "TEST-FRAGILITY THAT WILL WASTE ANOTHER AGENTS TIME - lifecycle_observability::ac_002_no_log_files_without_subscriber scans HOME-rooted measurement roots and false-positives on ANY concurrent write anywhere under HOME whose relative path merely CONTAINS the string fathomdb, including files in SIBLING repositories. Observed during Slice 20: it failed once claiming 'engine created a fathomdb-named artifact outside the DB path' and named /home/coreyt/projects/agent-aware/method/fathomdb-reference-census.md - a file in a different repo written by a concurrent process during the test window. Passed 3 of 3 on re-run and passes at baseline, so it is environmental, not a code defect. Because this machine routinely runs several agents across sibling repos at once, the false-positive rate is not negligible. Fix direction: scope the scan to the test's own temp/DB root rather than to HOME, or match the engine artifact naming precisely instead of a substring.", "area": "tooling/tests", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-58", "priority": "p3", "status": "open", "refs": ["slice:0.8.20-20b"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T00:46:17.646Z", "seq": 86, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "BLOCKER FOR SLICES 20c/25/30 - the governed-surface pin gate makes 'record the delta as a proposal AND land' mechanically impossible. REISSUE of the entry mis-filed as TC-54 at seq 81 (id race - TC-54 was already taken by the markdownlint observation at seq 80). plan-0.8.20.md section 11 ruling 2 (HITL 2026-07-25) says new governed surface at 20/25/30 is recorded as a proposal, the branch stays green, and the accumulated delta goes to the HITL once at the Slice 30 to Slice 40 boundary. But check-governed-surface-pin.sh compares a sha256 AND a git-blob-sha1 over the RAW BYTES of src/conformance/governed-surface-allowlist.json, so ANY diff - including the _comment prose edit that IS the proposal convention used by Slices 5d/10b/15b/15d - hard-fails the gate. preflight.sh --landing invokes that gate as a HARD fail, so the slice cannot land. The gate header asserts that tripping it is 'precisely what lets Slices 20/25/30 proceed WITHOUT stopping for a per-slice sign-off'; as wired that claim is FALSE - tripping it blocks the land. Slice 20 was therefore SPLIT so part b (dense_readiness) adds ZERO governed commands and landed clean at 26b237c0; part c (flush_embeddings) is HELD. Options: (a) FIX THE TOOLING - give the pin a pending_delta block so the gate passes iff allowlist equals pinned union pending_delta, loudly reporting the unsigned set, preserving every guarantee including the locked recovery_denylist; (b) fresh HITL sign-off plus re-pin per slice, which contradicts the batching ruling; (c) reclassify per TC-55. Recommend (a). Escalated to the Steward by the Slice-20 orchestrator.", "area": "release/0.8.20/governed-surface", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-59", "priority": "p1", "status": "open", "refs": ["seq:81", "tc:TC-55", "git:26b237c0", "file:scripts/check-governed-surface-pin.sh"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T00:46:17.674Z", "seq": 87, "kind": "observation", "summary": "RESTORED STATE after an id collision - this id belongs to the markdownlint-cli2.jsonc item, NOT to the governed-surface pin blocker. Original TC-54 (seq 80) recorded: .markdownlint-cli2.jsonc ignoring .claude/** is INTENTIONAL and stays, RESOLVED BY HITL 2026-07-25; that remains the state of TC-54 and it stays CLOSED. The Slice-20 orchestrator appended a different item under this same id at seq 81 after reading the ledger tail when TC-53 was the newest id and main then advanced with a new TC-54 before the write. Because state is the fold-to-latest per id, seq 81 had silently overwritten this item. That content is REISSUED as TC-59; this entry restores TC-54. Root cause worth fixing in TOOLING per the standing rule: ledgerwrite mints no id and cannot detect that a caller-supplied id already exists, so a read-then-write race across concurrent agents reuses ids silently - ledgerwrite should reject a NEW id that is already present unless an explicit update flag is passed. Related to TC-53, which records the adjacent shell-substitution corruption hazard in the same tool.", "area": "tooling/markdown", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-54", "priority": "p3", "status": "closed", "refs": ["seq:80", "seq:81", "tc:TC-59", "tc:TC-53"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T16:49:43.234Z", "seq": 88, "kind": "observation", "summary": "A third-party READ-WRITE SQLite connection opened against a LIVE engine database wedges the projection pipeline. Measured during Slice 20c X1 with node:sqlite DatabaseSync(path) in its default read-write mode: with an RW observer opened and closed between write and configure_projections, the declaration enrolment and backfill never became visible or effective, the subsequent drain timed out into SchedulerError, and _fathomdb_vector_kinds was still empty. Opening the same observer with readOnly true reproduces NONE of it. IMPORTANT and load-bearing: the R-20-DR barrier invariant HELD throughout - drain returned an ERROR, never a false Ok - so this is a robustness and WAL-interaction question, not a correctness violation of the flush barrier. Nothing in the Slice 20c fix causes it; the harness merely surfaced it. Any future test harness or operator tool that inspects a live engine DB must open READ-ONLY. Consider whether the engine should detect or document this.", "area": "engine/projection-runtime", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-60", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:7de05aae", "plan:dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T16:49:59.982Z", "seq": 89, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "CAVEAT for every future path that enqueues projection work: drain never calls notify_new_work, so an enqueue that does not itself notify leaves the dispatcher asleep on pending_scan false and drain burns its ENTIRE timeout into EngineError::Scheduler. Confirmed live during Slice 20c - an early cut enrolled the vector kind AFTER collect_projection_jobs had already decided whether to wake the dispatcher, and the row sat enqueued in the database with the dispatcher asleep for the full 120s. The api-surface.md C4 rider deliberately forbids making drain a trigger - drain is a barrier, wait-for-idle - so the fix ALWAYS belongs on the enqueue side. Slice 20c fixed the paths it owns (configure_projections notifies after commit; late enrolment runs before collect_projection_jobs). ANY OTHER enqueue path that does not notify retains this defect. Rule of thumb for future slices: enqueue and notify are one operation, never two.", "area": "engine/projection-runtime", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-61", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:7de05aae", "tc:TC-56"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T16:50:00.006Z", "seq": 90, "kind": "observation", "summary": "drain spins to its full timeout while ProjectionRuntime state.frozen is true. The dispatcher will not schedule while frozen, so a drain issued during a purge, erase, or rebuild window cannot make progress and returns EngineError::Scheduler rather than flushing. Freeze sites are in fathomdb-engine/src/lib.rs around the purge and erase paths and in run_rebuild. Found during Slice 20c and deliberately NOT fixed there - drain passivity is protected by the api-surface.md C4 rider and the internal callers already drain BEFORE freezing. Worth deciding whether drain should return a distinct typed error while frozen instead of an indistinguishable timeout, since a caller cannot currently tell a frozen engine from genuinely slow embedding.", "area": "engine/projection-runtime", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-62", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:7de05aae"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T16:50:10.059Z", "seq": 91, "kind": "observation", "summary": "run_rebuild can return with re-enqueued embed work still outstanding. It drains BEFORE rebuild_shadow_state rewinds the projection cursor, and never drains after, so the rebuild replays work onto the runtime and then returns while that work is still in flight. It relies on set_frozen(false) setting pending_scan to get the dispatcher moving. Found during Slice 20c and not fixed there (out of slice scope). Consequence for callers: an operator rebuild is not a synchronous flush - a caller that needs vectors materialised must issue its own drain AFTER run_rebuild returns. Related to TC-61 (enqueue and notify coupling).", "area": "engine/projection-runtime", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-63", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:7de05aae", "tc:TC-61"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T17:35:43.760Z", "seq": 92, "kind": "observation", "summary": "ProjectionRuntime::wait_for_idle has a lost-wakeup window that causes spurious slow drains and is a spurious-timeout risk for callers passing a short timeout. It releases the state lock to run database_has_pending_projection_work; a worker that finishes the last job inside that window notifies BEFORE the waiter re-acquires the lock, so the waiter misses the notification and sleeps the full remaining timeout before returning Ok. Measured during Slice 20c fix-1: the flush-barrier suite reported roughly 30s wall time with all assertions passing in about 40 percent of parallel runs (30.19s / 0.25s / 0.26s / 30.15s for the same test). Mechanism was confirmed by capping the cvar wait at 25ms - 6 of 6 runs then fast - and that one-line change was REVERTED because drain passivity is protected by the api-surface.md C4 rider and was out of the fix scope. PRE-EXISTING, not introduced by 20c; 20c only widened exposure by adding engines to the suite. A caller passing a short drain timeout can get a spurious Err(Scheduler) purely from this race. Fixing it means re-checking the predicate under the lock or bounding the cvar wait.", "area": "engine/projection-runtime", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-64", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:0759715a", "tc:TC-61"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T17:35:43.787Z", "seq": 93, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "CAVEAT for the slice that lands PER-ATTRIBUTE embedding: ProjectionSpec has NO kind axis, so Slice 20c had to guess the enrolment scope and the guess is load-bearing. The spec is name plus roles plus optional fts and vector sub-objects - per-ATTRIBUTE, with no kind - while derive_dense_readiness is documented as CORPUS-WIDE because 15d defers per-attribute embedding. Slice 20c therefore read a searchable-to-vector declaration as the corpus dense arm is live and enrolled every vector-eligible node kind present in canonical_nodes (its decision D1). That breadth directly produced BOTH codex round-2 findings: a P1 permanent wedge when a corpus contains a kind outside the resolve_source_type locked list, and a P2 false-ready on the late-enrolment path. Both were fixed in 20c fix-2, but the underlying seam remains: enrolment scope is inferred, not declared. When per-attribute embedding lands, the enrolment scoping and the readiness scoping must move together through the SAME seam, and this inference should be replaced by an explicit kind axis rather than re-derived.", "area": "engine/projection-registry", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-65", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:0759715a", "tc:TC-55"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T17:48:42.336Z", "seq": 94, "kind": "observation", "summary": "TC-11 logical_id pin: the STATIC migration guard is a lexical scanner, not a SQL parser — known-open evasion shapes remain; the DYNAMIC whole-ladder test (D2) is the load-bearing, shape-independent proof", "area": "schema-migrations", "epistemic": "verified", "id": "TC-66", "owner": "unassigned", "priority": "P3", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:83b1c818", "plan:dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md"], "body": "Landed at 0.8.20 Slice 25 (R-20-SUR). check_migration_logical_id_pin in src/rust/crates/fathomdb-schema/src/lib.rs statically rejects any migration step that would populate logical_id on an existing canonical row. It is a LEXICAL scanner (comment-strip, string-literal elision, quoted-identifier unwrap, schema-qualifier normalisation, statement split on \";\") and therefore cannot be complete against SQL grammar. codex §9 produced FOUR same-family [P2] evasions across three fix rounds — schema-qualified names, UPDATE OR <conflict-action>, leading-CTE, and subquery-in-an-earlier-SET-expression. The first three are CLOSED; the fourth and the shapes below are KNOWN-OPEN and recorded in the function doc comment under \"# Known limits\": subquery in an earlier SET expression truncating the SET clause, a rename round-trip, and a CREATE TABLE AS SELECT swap. The run halted at the orchestration circuit-breaker (3 fix rounds on one finding) — a 4th round buys a 5th [P2]; Steward ruled ACCEPT the residual. WHY THAT IS SAFE: the requirement is NOT proven by the static guard. It is proven by the dynamic guard in fathomdb-schema/tests/slice25_logical_id_pin.rs — migrating_to_head_never_populates_logical_id_on_an_existing_node/_edge snapshot identity ROWS before and after migrating a real mixed anonymous/governed corpus through the whole ladder to head, and assert via fn null_to_not_null that NULL -> NOT NULL transitions == 0 plus byte-identical IdSpace::to_prefixed(). That compares rows, not SQL text, so it catches EVERY evasion the lexical guard misses, whatever its shape. D1 is defence-in-depth and a fast authoring-time fail; D2 is the proof. DURABLE CAVEAT FOR A FUTURE SLICE/RELEASE: do not treat a green static guard as evidence the pin holds, and do not delete or weaken the D2 whole-ladder tests to speed up the schema suite — they are the only shape-independent enforcement. If anyone wants the static guard to be actually complete, the durable fix is to parse the migration SQL with a real SQLite grammar rather than extend the lexical scanner; that is a net-new dependency and was explicitly out of scope for 0.8.20."}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T18:35:30.715Z", "seq": 95, "kind": "open-question", "summary": "OPEN QUESTION for the HITL and for consumer adoption: the dense arm is now explicitly scoped to the resolve_source_type LOCKED vocabulary - email, article, paper, meeting, note, todo, doc, plus edge_fact. Slice 20c fix-2 closed a codex P1 by restricting vector-kind enrolment to commit-able kinds (kind_is_vector_committable delegates to resolve_source_type(kind).is_ok(), so the two cannot drift). The alternative - teaching the vector writer arbitrary node kinds - was NOT taken because it reaches into Pack-1 partition-key semantics locked by the HITL in dev/design/0.7.0-vector-quant-pack1.md D3. CONSEQUENCE a consumer must know: a workspace using entity kinds outside that list (Memex, for example) gets FTS and lexical search for them but SILENTLY NO VECTORS - no typed error, no failure row, and readiness still reports ready, which is correct because an unenrolled kind is not outstanding work. This is that kind pre-slice status quo restored, not a new regression, but 20c is the first release where declaring a vector projection makes the boundary observable. The question to settle: should the source-type vocabulary grow, should it become consumer-extensible, or should declaring a vector projection over an unsupported kind report something rather than be silent? Growing it is a Pack-1 decision under HITL lock, so a fix round may not take it.", "area": "engine/vector-projection", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-66", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:1b0cf514", "tc:TC-65"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T18:36:45.159Z", "seq": 96, "kind": "open-question", "summary": "REISSUE of the item mis-filed as TC-66 at seq 95 (id race with a concurrent Slice-25 orchestrator - TC-66 was already taken by the TC-11 migration-guard observation at seq 94). OPEN QUESTION for the HITL and for consumer adoption: the dense arm is now explicitly scoped to the resolve_source_type LOCKED vocabulary - email, article, paper, meeting, note, todo, doc, plus edge_fact. Slice 20c fix-2 closed a codex P1 by restricting vector-kind enrolment to commit-able kinds (kind_is_vector_committable delegates to resolve_source_type(kind).is_ok(), so the two cannot drift). The alternative - teaching the vector writer arbitrary node kinds - was NOT taken because it reaches into Pack-1 partition-key semantics locked by the HITL in dev/design/0.7.0-vector-quant-pack1.md D3. CONSEQUENCE a consumer must know: a workspace using entity kinds outside that list (Memex, for example) gets FTS and lexical search for them but SILENTLY NO VECTORS - no typed error, no failure row, and readiness still reports ready, which is correct because an unenrolled kind is not outstanding work. This is that kind pre-slice status quo restored, not a new regression, but 20c is the first release where declaring a vector projection makes the boundary observable. The question to settle: should the source-type vocabulary grow, should it become consumer-extensible, or should declaring a vector projection over an unsupported kind report something rather than be silent? Growing it is a Pack-1 decision under HITL lock, so a fix round may not take it.", "area": "engine/vector-projection", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-67", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:1b0cf514", "tc:TC-65", "seq:95"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T18:37:08.517Z", "seq": 97, "kind": "observation", "summary": "RESTORED STATE after an id collision - this id belongs to the TC-11 static-migration-guard observation from Slice 25 (R-20-SUR), NOT to the vector-kind vocabulary question. That content is REISSUED as TC-67. Root cause: the Slice-20c orchestrator verified TC-66 was free, and a CONCURRENT Slice-25 orchestrator appended this item at seq 94 before the 20c write at seq 95; because state is the fold-to-latest per id, seq 95 silently overwrote it. Same failure shape as the TC-54 collision at seq 81/87, now demonstrated to recur across CONCURRENT orchestrators rather than a stale tail read - re-checking immediately before the write is NOT sufficient. Tooling fix stands (TC-53/TC-54): ledgerwrite should reject a NEW caller-supplied id that already exists unless an explicit update flag is passed. Original state below, unchanged and still OPEN. TC-11 logical_id pin: the STATIC migration guard is a lexical scanner, not a SQL parser — known-open evasion shapes remain; the DYNAMIC whole-ladder test (D2) is the load-bearing, shape-independent proof", "area": "schema-migrations", "epistemic": "verified", "id": "TC-66", "owner": "unassigned", "priority": "P3", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:83b1c818", "plan:dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md", "seq:94", "seq:95", "tc:TC-67"], "body": "Landed at 0.8.20 Slice 25 (R-20-SUR). check_migration_logical_id_pin in src/rust/crates/fathomdb-schema/src/lib.rs statically rejects any migration step that would populate logical_id on an existing canonical row. It is a LEXICAL scanner (comment-strip, string-literal elision, quoted-identifier unwrap, schema-qualifier normalisation, statement split on \";\") and therefore cannot be complete against SQL grammar. codex §9 produced FOUR same-family [P2] evasions across three fix rounds — schema-qualified names, UPDATE OR <conflict-action>, leading-CTE, and subquery-in-an-earlier-SET-expression. The first three are CLOSED; the fourth and the shapes below are KNOWN-OPEN and recorded in the function doc comment under \"# Known limits\": subquery in an earlier SET expression truncating the SET clause, a rename round-trip, and a CREATE TABLE AS SELECT swap. The run halted at the orchestration circuit-breaker (3 fix rounds on one finding) — a 4th round buys a 5th [P2]; Steward ruled ACCEPT the residual. WHY THAT IS SAFE: the requirement is NOT proven by the static guard. It is proven by the dynamic guard in fathomdb-schema/tests/slice25_logical_id_pin.rs — migrating_to_head_never_populates_logical_id_on_an_existing_node/_edge snapshot identity ROWS before and after migrating a real mixed anonymous/governed corpus through the whole ladder to head, and assert via fn null_to_not_null that NULL -> NOT NULL transitions == 0 plus byte-identical IdSpace::to_prefixed(). That compares rows, not SQL text, so it catches EVERY evasion the lexical guard misses, whatever its shape. D1 is defence-in-depth and a fast authoring-time fail; D2 is the proof. DURABLE CAVEAT FOR A FUTURE SLICE/RELEASE: do not treat a green static guard as evidence the pin holds, and do not delete or weaken the D2 whole-ladder tests to speed up the schema suite — they are the only shape-independent enforcement. If anyone wants the static guard to be actually complete, the durable fix is to parse the migration SQL with a real SQLite grammar rather than extend the lexical scanner; that is a net-new dependency and was explicitly out of scope for 0.8.20."}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T19:52:57.605Z", "seq": 98, "kind": "observation", "summary": "MEASURED: reopening a database that has ANY enrolled vector kind costs 90 SYNCHRONOUS embeds inside Engine::open - the 0.8.18 vector-equivalence probe. This was previously unreachable in practice because nothing enrolled a node kind in production; Slice 20c gave _fathomdb_vector_kinds its FIRST production enrolment path (configure_projections declaring searchable-to-vector), so from 0.8.20 onward a normal consumer workspace can hit it on every open. Found while investigating the no-embedder enqueue question in 20c fix-3 and NOT fixed there (out of slice scope). Consequence: open latency becomes proportional to the equivalence-probe batch rather than constant, on a path consumers hit at startup. Worth deciding whether the probe should be sampled, made async, cached against a fingerprint, or skipped when the embedder identity is unchanged since the last open.", "area": "engine/open-path", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-68", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:2b162287", "tc:TC-65"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T19:52:57.633Z", "seq": 99, "kind": "observation", "summary": "The EDGE projection path has the same unconditional-enqueue shape as the node path that Slice 20c fixed, and the un-stranding helper does not reach it. project_canonical_edge_row enqueues unconditionally, while reenqueue_stranded_vector_rows joins canonical_nodes ONLY - so gating edges alone would merely trade one permanent terminal for another rather than making edge embed work recoverable. PRE-EXISTING since G11; NOT introduced by 20c and deliberately not fixed there (20c scoped itself to the node path its own enrolment change created). A future slice that wants edge bodies to be recoverable after a no-embedder or failed session must extend the stranded-row query to canonical_edges at the same time as it gates the edge enqueue - the two must land together or the fix is a no-op.", "area": "engine/projection-runtime", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-69", "priority": "p3", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:2b162287", "tc:TC-61"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T21:47:56.012Z", "seq": 100, "kind": "observation", "summary": "PRE-EXISTING SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT race between a projection worker committing its terminal and a caller open write, already documented in slice20_dense_readiness.rs. MEASURED during 20c fix-4 while probing whether the embed retry ladder could be shortened for edges: shortening ONLY the edge ladder moved the worker terminal-commit into the window of a caller write and took consolidate_provider from 0 of 48 failures to 8 of 48 under 6-way concurrency (origin-main baseline is 0 of 48). The retry ladder was therefore LEFT AS SHIPPED and the race was not fixed - 20c deliberately did not touch it. CAVEAT for any future slice that wants to shorten, remove, or make adaptive the projection retry ladder (DEFAULT_PROJECTION_RETRY_DELAYS_MS): the ladder is currently acting as an accidental backoff that masks this race, so changing it WILL surface SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT failures under concurrency. Fix the race first, then the ladder - not the other way round.", "area": "engine/concurrency", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-70", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:1f46438b", "tc:TC-64"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-26T22:43:48.228Z", "seq": 101, "kind": "todo", "summary": "OPEN codex section-9 [P2] from Slice 20c round 5, DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED - the orchestration circuit-breaker was reached at 5 fix rounds. vector_projection_declared keys off the stored vector sub-object ALONE (vector_declared = 1) rather than requiring the searchable role, so declaring {roles: [filterable], vector: true} in a session WITH a live embedder will enrol vector kinds, backfill, and make later writes enqueue embeddings - even though that combination is documented as inert-but-round-trippable (the shipped Slice-15d contract pinned by vector_subobject_is_stored_not_built). The shipped inert test does not catch it because it runs without a live embedder. FIX: make the predicate require the searchable role, i.e. use the same active searchable-to-vector predicate everywhere. NOTE the blast radius - vector_projection_declared gates all three of the forward backfill path, the drop inverse, and late enrolment, so the change must be re-verified across all of them together. IMPACT is wasted embed work and unexpected vectors at rest for a projection meant to be inert; it is NOT a false-ready and NOT data loss, which is why it was judged lower severity than the four findings closed in rounds 1-4. Transcript: dev/plans/runs/codex/0.8.20/slice-20c-fix-5-rereview-20260726T224040Z.log", "area": "engine/projection-registry", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-71", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:30ab62df", "tc:TC-65"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-27T00:00:11.891Z", "seq": 102, "kind": "observation", "summary": "MEASURED: cargo test --workspace --no-fail-fast is NOT reliably green on this machine, on main as well as on a slice branch, so a single green workspace run is WEAK evidence. Controlled comparison run during the Slice 20c landing gate, same script, sequential, same load: the merged 20c tree failed 1 of 3 runs and plain main failed 1 of 3 runs. Each failure was a DIFFERENT concurrency-sensitive test - slice15e undeclared_after_concurrent_drop_is_typed_invalidfilter_not_storage_race on the merged side, t_s34_dump_mutations_lock_held_exits_71 on main, and lifecycle_observability in an earlier ad-hoc run - and EVERY one of them passes reliably in isolation (slice15e 6 of 6, lifecycle_observability 3 of 3). Conclusion: this is parallel-execution contention in the full-workspace run, NOT a regression from any one slice, and it is the same family as TC-70. CONSEQUENCE for future slices: do not treat a single red full-workspace run as a blocker without re-running the failing suite in isolation and comparing against a main control, and do not treat a single green run as proof. If the workspace gate is to be load-bearing for release DoD it needs the underlying races fixed or the run serialised.", "area": "testing/ci", "decider": "orchestrator", "id": "TC-72", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:841c307b", "tc:TC-70"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-27T00:03:17.543Z", "seq": 103, "kind": "todo", "summary": "SLICE 21 SCOPE PLACED (HITL 2026-07-26) — a reserved-gap slice in the 20 band, off the critical path to Slice 30, to land BEFORE publish. Two items, both engine/test hygiene. (1) TC-57 characterize-then-fix per the ruling at steward seq-111: governed writes (logical_id Some) racing the projection worker return EngineError::Storage, reported 7 of 8 baseline runs, never with logical_id None, suspected SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT; reproduce and characterize FIRST, then fix in-release. (2) ac_002 ORACLE REPLACEMENT, not an engine fix and not a new test. Triage verdict: ac_002 and lifecycle_observability.rs are the SAME finding (the file contains the test) so this was two flakes not three, and both flakes are class (A) harness artifacts with NO engine defect and NO TC-57 corroboration — ac_029 writes logical_id None while TC-57 is logical_id Some, a different failure mode. ac_002 Part 1 (allowlist inside the DB parent) is hermetic and good, KEEP IT. Part 2 scans PWD/HOME/XDG_*/TMPDIR to depth 6 for any relative path containing the substring fathomdb: it is simultaneously TOO BROAD (fails on files other processes create; already false-positived twice, once on a sibling repo file, TC-58) and TOO NARROW (misses a log at ~/.cache/engine.log or anything without fathomdb in its path or deeper than 6 levels) — so the AC protecting users from a littered filesystem has a false-negative hole. Fix by isolation not detection: redirect HOME/XDG_*/TMPDIR/CWD into a per-test sandbox and assert the sandbox holds nothing but the allowlist, which also closes the false-negative. Prefer a child process with a scrubbed env since std::env::set_var is racy across parallel test threads. DO NOT patch the substring/root-stripping logic a third time — it has already been patched twice (0.8.9 Slice 20 F-9, then the longest-matching-root fix) and that is the Slice-25 lexical-guard lesson: when an oracle keeps producing new evasions, change the oracle.", "decider": "hitl", "id": "TC-73", "status": "open", "refs": ["seq:111", "seq:114"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-27T14:29:49.663Z", "seq": 104, "kind": "decision", "summary": "WORKSPACE-GATE EVIDENCE RULE RULED (HITL 2026-07-27): option (a) - SERIALIZE the Slice-40 workspace test gate AND keep a NON-BLOCKING parallel run that reports without gating. Race-hunting itself defers to 0.8.21. Chosen over (b) serialize-only because (b) buys the same determinism while destroying the only signal that these races exist, and TC-57 already proves at least one race in this family is a REAL engine defect rather than a harness artifact. Basis at seq-116: the full-workspace run failed 1 of 3 on merged-20c AND 1 of 3 on plain main, a different concurrency test each time, every one green in isolation - so today a single green proves nothing and a single red blocks nothing. Serializing buys DETERMINISM, not correctness: the races stop being exercised rather than being fixed, which is why the non-blocking arm is load-bearing and not decoration. WORK OWED at Slice 40. UNMEASURED and worth measuring before committing: serialized wall-clock. NOT COVERED by this ruling and still unclassified: OOS-12 slice15e undeclared-after-concurrent-drop has NOT been triaged harness-vs-real and sits on the registry path 0.8.20 publishes.", "decider": "hitl", "id": "TC-74", "priority": "p2", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "40", "status": "open", "refs": ["tc:TC-72", "tc:TC-16", "seq:116"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-27T14:29:49.691Z", "seq": 105, "kind": "decision", "summary": "ENGINE FIX-ROUND CAP RULED (HITL 2026-07-27): option (a) - the numbers are CONFIRMED as same-finding bound STAYS AT 3, per-slice TOTAL rises 6 to 10 for slices touching fathomdb-engine/src, with a MANDATORY STEWARD CHECK-IN AT 6 at which the orchestrator reports what each round found and the Steward decides continue / re-scope / escalate. Chosen over (c) no-fixed-total-with-judgement because an unbounded total removes the runaway backstop that caught TC-47, and the check-in already supplies the judgement (c) was reaching for. STEWARD DISCLOSURE MADE BEFORE THE RULING: these numbers were the Stewards invention, agreed to rather than authored by the HITL, and the disclosure was made while overturning was still cheap. Basis: in Slice 20c the same-finding rule NEVER fired - every round found a new distinct defect, each RED-first - so the binding constraint was the total and the breaker fired on a productive loop. WORK OWED: this rule is NOT YET IN FORCE. The durable homes still read 3 and 6 - dev/design/orchestration.md section 6 and dev/plans/prompts/0.8.x-RELEASE-ORCHESTRATOR-HANDOFF.md lines 159 to 163 - so every orchestrator commissioned before that lands gets the OLD bound. Amends seq-107 for engine slices only; supersedes nothing else in that rule.", "decider": "hitl", "id": "TC-75", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["seq:107", "seq:117"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-27T14:29:49.714Z", "seq": 106, "kind": "decision", "summary": "LIBRARY SLICE REQUIREMENT-ID RULED (HITL 2026-07-27): option (a) - Slice 31 Library Sweep 3 carries NO R-20-xx requirement id and is annotated explicitly as dependency hygiene and as a DELIBERATE DEPARTURE from the master F-12 pico precedent. Chosen over (b) mint R-20-LIB because (b) would convert a RECURRING hygiene programme into a one-off release requirement of 0.8.20, which is the category error F-12 was created to fix; the annotation preserves the precedent for the next Steward instead of silently overwriting it. Context: F-12 and the two-tier numbering model treat a Library Sweep as a PICO, label-only and never published, so placing this one in-ladder before publish is the departure being recorded. RE-OPEN TRIGGER: if the sqlite-vec PR 99 probe promotes that dependency into this slice, it stops being pure hygiene and option (b) becomes correct.", "decider": "hitl", "id": "TC-76", "release": "0.8.20", "slice": "31", "status": "open", "refs": ["tc:TC-73"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-27T14:29:49.736Z", "seq": 107, "kind": "decision", "summary": "RESERVED-GAP OVERFLOW TRIPWIRE RULED (HITL 2026-07-27): option (a) FOR NOW - keep the plan section 5 default, which halts to the HITL only on band overflow. Explicitly CONDITIONAL: the HITL ruled a for now and stated it DEPENDS ON WHETHER SLICES 21 AND 22 TURN OUT TO NEED MORE SLICES. Chosen over (b) halt-at-the-next-new-slice because the 20 band was sized as slack deliberately and (b) spends HITL attention on precisely what section 5 exists to handle without them; the growth so far came from HITL rulings, not from drift, so the tripwire is not the mechanism that has been failing. STATE AT RULING: the 20 band gaps 21 to 24 now hold 21 and 22, leaving TWO slots. RE-OPEN TRIGGER, and the Steward is to raise it unprompted: if Slice 21 or Slice 22 spawns two or more further slices, option (b) becomes correct and this returns to the HITL.", "decider": "hitl", "id": "TC-77", "priority": "p3", "release": "0.8.20", "status": "open", "refs": ["plan:dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md"]}
{"ts": "2026-07-27T14:29:49.758Z", "seq": 108, "kind": "decision", "summary": "DEPENDABOT PR 153 setup-node 6 to 7 RULED (HITL 2026-07-27): option (a) FOR NOW - terminal hold, revisit after publish with the rest of the held set, with the HITL directing MONITOR FOR OPTION (c). No analysis is scheduled before publish. Chosen over (c) fold-into-the-Slice-40-publish-rehearsal because the npm leg published successfully at v0.8.9 on the CURRENT action version, so there is no evidence we are broken today, and (c) would add an unmeasured variable to the one rehearsal that most needs to be clean. UNRESOLVED AND DELIBERATELY LEFT SO: a subagent reads v7 as plausibly FIXING our npm OIDC publish path rather than threatening it, because v6 exports a placeholder NODE_AUTH_TOKEN that can stop npm attempting the OIDC exchange - that reading is ANALYSIS, NOT MEASUREMENT, on a path that has never run. MONITOR TRIGGER per the HITL: if the Slice-40 publish rehearsal fails on npm auth, option (c) becomes the diagnosis rather than a precaution, and this re-opens immediately.", "decider": "hitl", "id": "TC-78", "priority": "p3", "release": "0.8.20", "status": "open"}
{"ts": "2026-07-27T15:28:44.350Z", "seq": 109, "kind": "observation", "summary": "test_commission_manifest.sh arm 9d is RED on main today, and commission-manifest is an ALWAYS-ON CI job", "area": "scripts/tests", "epistemic": "verified", "id": "TC-79", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:23a436dc", "slice:0.8.20-30"], "body": "MEASURED during Slice 30, twice, independently. scripts/tests/test_commission_manifest.sh exits 1 on arm 9d with: FAIL arm 9d (real base line): base=[ base sha a2022957 (Slice 15 - the newest LANDED slice STRICTLY BEFORE Slice 20)]. Reproduced by the implementer in a throwaway clone at baseline 23a436dc, and reproduced again by the orchestrator in the PRIMARY checkout with main at 23a436dc and zero slice-30 changes applied. Slice 30 does not touch scripts/commission-manifest.sh or its test (git diff --stat over the slice range for both paths is empty), so this is PRE-EXISTING and not slice-caused. WHY IT MATTERS: commission-manifest is one of the always-on CI jobs in .github/workflows/ci.yml (no if:, no needs:), and it runs BOTH the generator with --verify-all AND this fixture suite as a recurrence guard. So CI on main is presumably red on this job right now. The arm expects the manifest generator to name the newest LANDED slice strictly before the next slice; the ladder has since been re-sequenced (30 runs first of the remainder, with 21/22/31 net-new), so the likely cause is that the re-sequence moved what next-slice and base resolve to and the fixture arm was not updated with it. NEEDS A HOME: it is not in any slice scope today. Natural candidates are the Slice 40 verification sweep (alongside the TC-16 determination, which is the same category of dead or stale release-gate assertion) or a DOC-HYGIENE pass. DO NOT let a green Slice-30 landing be read as evidence this is fixed."}
{"ts": "2026-07-27T15:28:58.568Z", "seq": 110, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "C-1 contract Q6(b) carries a stale parenthetical its OWN amendment banner contradicts - fix it at the NEXT amendment, never on its own", "area": "dev/design/record-lifecycle-protocol", "epistemic": "verified", "id": "TC-80", "priority": "p3", "status": "open", "refs": ["git:efa8d584", "slice:0.8.20-30", "file:dev/design/record-lifecycle-protocol/OPP-12-C1-converged-contract.md"], "body": "Found while deriving the R-20-H7 clause registry. Inside clause Q6(b) of OPP-12-C1-converged-contract.md the parenthetical says the C-2 typed carrier had its additive stable_id base landed but its typed swap NOT STARTED. Four lines below, the TC-11 amendment banner added at efa8d584 says the C-2 swap SHIPPED ALONE in 0.8.19 and is total without a surrogate. As-built agrees with the BANNER: SearchHit.id is a non-Option IdSpace (fathomdb-engine/src/lib.rs around line 1575) and IdSpaceKind is a closed three-variant enum. So the parenthetical is a point-in-time status note that its own clause has since superseded. IT IS NOT A GATE FAILURE and was deliberately registered in scripts/c1-conformance-pin.json as category PROSE, id C1-Q6B-C2-CARRIER-STATUS, with that reason written into the pin. Pinning it CHECKABLE would have held the breaking-pair publish on an already-superseded sentence - the exact hazard the efa8d584 amendment was made to remove. THE CAVEAT FOR WHOEVER FIXES IT: the R-20-H7 gate pins the sha256 AND git blob sha1 of this contract file. ANY edit to it, including a one-word prose correction, trips the gate and REQUIRES re-deriving the whole 45-clause registry against the new text and re-pinning under review. That is correct-by-design, not a bug. Therefore do NOT fix this parenthetical as a standalone docs commit - fold it into the next substantive contract amendment, where the re-derivation has to happen anyway. Same shape as the TC-52 allowlist _comment deferral."}
{"ts": "2026-07-27T15:29:19.891Z", "seq": 111, "kind": "caveat", "summary": "R-20-H7 gate maintenance contract: what it does NOT cover, the permanently-red assertion hazard, and the fixture coupling it introduced", "area": "scripts", "epistemic": "verified", "id": "TC-81", "priority": "p2", "status": "open", "refs": ["slice:0.8.20-30", "file:scripts/check-c1-conformance.sh", "file:scripts/c1-conformance-pin.json"], "body": "Three durable properties of the can-i-deploy gate landed at Slice 30 that a later agent must not rediscover the hard way. (1) THE PERMANENTLY-RED ASSERTION HAZARD IS A CLASS, not a one-off. A can-i-deploy assertion keyed to a release action that has NOT HAPPENED YET holds the release forever. It bit this contract once already: pre-efa8d584 clause Q6(b) mandated anonymous surrogate minting that the ratified TC-11 pin forbids ever implementing. Slice 30 avoided three MORE instances of the same class: (a) the contract Landing section says C-1 co-lands at 0.8.20, but the manifests still read 0.8.9 until the Slice-40 bump, so the gate asserts C-1 CODE PRESENT ON THE 0.8.20 LINE and deliberately NOT a manifest version equality; (b) the per-projection vector embedder override is RECORDED BUT INERT (one engine-wide pipeline serves all vector projections), so the gate asserts only absent-implies-engine-default; (c) fts tokenizer override likewise recorded but deliberately not honoured, pinned to the 0.9.x multi-field FTS work. DO NOT TIGHTEN ANY OF THESE THREE into the stronger nearby statement. (2) THE GATE IS STATIC-STRUCTURAL AND SAYS SO. It asserts symbols, tables, negative-space greps, and for behavioural clauses that the NAMED TEST still exists - it does NOT re-execute those suites. Consequence worth naming: TC-71 (vector_projection_declared ignores the searchable role) breaks NO verbatim C-1 clause, so when Slice 21 fixes it this gate will neither notice nor object. Do not read a green H7 as evidence about TC-71 either way. (3) FIXTURE COUPLING INTRODUCED DELIBERATELY. Wiring the gate into preflight --landing turned four sibling suites red (test_preflight_landing, test_check_ledgers, test_check_board_currency, test_check_governed_surface_pin) because their throwaway fixture repos carry no C-1 subject and the gate correctly exited 2 under anti-fail-open. Repaired with a seeder scripts/tests/lib/c1-conformance-fixture.sh that COPIES the real artifacts, unlike its synthetic sibling lib/governed-surface-fixture.sh. That departure is forced, not lazy: the implemented-assertion set is a constant inside the gate and the registry bijection is rigid in both directions, so any pin not registering exactly the 26 CHECKABLE ids reports MALFORMED. If the clause registry ever changes, those four suites inherit the change through the seeder - expect it and do not weaken the gate to decouple them."}
